window.INFRASIM_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Containers and Images: Your First Container Before you can fix a container, you need a clear picture of what one is. This lesson builds that picture and has you running, reaching and removing containers within ten minutes. ⏱ 30 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand what a container and an image are, and run your first containers in the sandbox. Why it matters: almost every Docker and Kubernetes problem you will ever debug comes down to \"which process is running, from which image, with which settings?\". If the mental model is right, the error messages start making sense."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain the difference between an image and a container. Run a container in the foreground and in the background. Publish a container port and reach it from the host. List, stop and remove containers."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation You joined a team whose application \"runs in Docker\". On your first day somebody says: \"just run the web container and check it on port 8080\". You have a terminal and no idea what happens when you press Enter. Let's fix that first."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"what-a-container-is-and-is-not","h":"What a container is (and is not)","x":"What a container is (and is not) A container is an ordinary Linux process that the kernel has been told to isolate. It gets its own view of the file system, its own network interfaces, its own process list and its own host name. It is not a small virtual machine: there is no second operating system kernel and nothing \"boots\". Virtual machine Container Isolates A whole machine, with its own kernel One process (and its children), sharing the host's kernel Starts in Tens of seconds Milliseconds Lives as long as You keep it switched on Its main process keeps running Contains An operating system installation Only the files the application needs The rule that explains most surprises A container exists to run one main process . When that process exits, the container stops. If the process crashes at startup, the container \"runs\" for a fraction of a second. You will use this rule in almost every fix-it scenario of this course."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"images-the-template-a-container-starts-from","h":"Images: the template a container starts from","x":"Images: the template a container starts from An image is a read-only package: a file system (the application, its runtime, its libraries) plus metadata such as the command to start. A container is a running instance of an image. One image can back any number of containers, just as one program on disk can be started many times. Images are identified by a repository name and a tag , and they are stored in registries : docker.io / library / nginx : 1.29 └ registry └ namespace └ repository └ tag nginx means docker.io/library/nginx:latest shop/web:1.0 means docker.io/shop/web:1.0 If you leave out the tag, Docker assumes latest . That is only a tag name like any other; it does not mean \"newest\". Naming an explicit version ( nginx:1.29 ) makes it obvious what is running, which matters a great deal once you are debugging."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"docker-run-step-by-step","h":"docker run, step by step","x":"docker run, step by step docker run hello-world This one command does three separate things, and each can fail in its own way: Pull : the image is not on this machine yet, so Docker downloads it from the registry ( Unable to find image … locally , then Pulling from … ). Create : Docker creates a container from the image: a writable layer on top of the image's files, a network interface, a name. Start : Docker starts the image's command as the container's main process and connects your terminal to its output. hello-world prints a message and exits, so the container stops again immediately. That is the rule from above in action."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"a-container-that-keeps-running","h":"A container that keeps running","x":"A container that keeps running docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx:1.29 Part Meaning -d Detached: run in the background and print the container ID instead of the output. --name web A name you choose. Without it Docker invents one, like quirky_turing . -p 8080:80 Publish: connections to port 8080 on the host are forwarded to port 80 in the container . nginx:1.29 The image. Everything after it would be a command to run instead of the image's default. docker ps # running containers curl localhost:8080 # talk to nginx through the published port CONTAINER ID IMAGE COMMAND CREATED STATUS PORTS NAMES 8aefd9ff8d08 nginx:1.29 \"/docker-entrypoint.…\" 5 seconds ago Up 4 seconds 0.0.0.0:8080->80/tcp, [::]:8080->80/tcp web Read the PORTS column from left to right: host address and port, an arrow, container port. You will come back to this column often. Why the port must be published The container has its own network interface with its own address. Nginx is listening on port 80 of that interface , which the outside world cannot reach. -p asks Docker to open a port on the host and forward it. Forget -p and the server runs perfectly, and nobody can reach it."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"stopping-and-removing","h":"Stopping and removing","x":"Stopping and removing docker stop web # ask the main process to exit; the container still exists docker ps -a # -a also lists stopped containers docker rm web # delete the container (its writable layer is gone) docker images # the image is still on disk, ready for the next run ❌ A common confusion \"I stopped the container, why can't I run a new one with the same name?\" Because a stopped container still exists. docker ps hides it; docker ps -a shows it. ✅ What to do Remove it ( docker rm web ), or start the existing one again ( docker start web ). For throw-away containers add --rm to docker run , and Docker removes the container when it exits."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"scenario-your-first-containers","h":"Scenario: your first containers","x":"Scenario: your first containers Time to do it yourself. Press Start the sandbox , then work through the tasks. The sandbox watches what you do and ticks off each task. If a command is new to you, type it anyway: nothing here can break anything. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript. About the sandbox The terminal is a simulation that runs in this page. It behaves like a Linux shell with Docker installed: ↑ recalls earlier commands, Tab completes names, Ctrl + C stops a command that is waiting. Type help to see everything it understands. Reset starts the scenario from scratch."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A container is an isolated process, not a small virtual machine. It lives exactly as long as its main process. An image is the read-only template; a container is a running instance with a thin writable layer on top. docker run = pull (if needed) + create + start. -p HOST:CONTAINER publishes a port. Without it the container is unreachable from the host. docker ps shows running containers; docker ps -a shows all of them. Stopped is not the same as removed."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz You run docker run -d --name job alpine echo done . A second later docker ps shows nothing. Why? A. The image could not be downloaded B. The main process ( echo ) finished, so the container stopped C. -d deletes the container after it starts D. Alpine containers can't run in the background A container lives as long as its main process. echo prints and exits, so the container is now in the Exited state, visible with docker ps -a . What does -p 8080:80 do? A. Opens port 8080 inside the container and port 80 on the host B. Limits the container to ports 80 through 8080 C. Forwards host port 8080 to port 80 inside the container D. Starts 80 copies of the container The format is HOST:CONTAINER. The left side is where you connect from outside, the right side is where the process inside listens. After docker stop web , which statement is true? A. The container still exists and can be started again with docker start web B. The container and its image are deleted C. The container's name is free to be reused immediately D. The container keeps running without a network Stopping ends the main process. The container, its settings and its writable layer remain until docker rm ."},{"l":1,"s":"01-first-container","t":"Containers and Images: Your First Container","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Docker overview docker container run reference What is an image?"},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect Three commands answer almost every question about a container: what did it say, what does it look like from the inside, and how was it configured. This lesson teaches them, and then uses them on a database that refuses to stay up. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn the container lifecycle and the three commands that explain a container's behaviour: docker logs , docker exec and docker inspect . Why it matters: troubleshooting is collecting evidence. These commands are where the evidence is. Engineers who reach for them first fix problems in minutes; those who guess restart things for hours."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Name the states a container moves through and what causes each transition. Read a container's output and its exit code. Run commands and open a shell inside a running container. Find a container's configuration with docker inspect . Diagnose a container that exits immediately after starting."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation A container is running on a machine you have just been handed. Nobody remembers how it was started. Later, a second container is started and vanishes from docker ps within a second. Both situations are solved with the same small toolkit."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"the-lifecycle","h":"The lifecycle","x":"The lifecycle docker create docker start main process exits │ │ │ ▼ ▼ ▼ Created ───────────────► Running ───────────────────► Exited (code) ▲ │ docker stop / kill │ │ └──────────────────────────────┤ └────────── docker start ──────────┘ │ docker rm ▼ (gone) docker run is create + start . The STATUS column of docker ps -a shows the state, and for an exited container the exit code of its main process: CONTAINER ID IMAGE COMMAND CREATED STATUS PORTS NAMES 1abf541becdf postgres:17 \"docker-entrypoint.s…\" 2 minutes ago Exited (1) 2 minutes ago db 247720201ef0 shop/web:1.0 \"docker-entrypoint.s…\" 10 minutes ago Up 10 minutes ... web Exit code Usual meaning 0 The process finished successfully. Normal for one-off jobs, suspicious for a server. 1 (or another small number) The application reported an error. The reason is in the logs. 126 / 127 The command could not be executed / was not found in the image. 137 Killed with SIGKILL (128 + 9): docker kill , a stop that timed out, or the out-of-memory killer. 143 Terminated with SIGTERM (128 + 15): a normal docker stop of a process that exits on the signal."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"docker-logs-what-did-it-say","h":"docker logs: what did it say?","x":"docker logs: what did it say? Docker captures everything the main process writes to standard output and standard error. That stream is the container's log, and it is kept even after the container has exited. docker logs web # everything so far docker logs --tail 20 web # the last 20 lines docker logs -f web # follow new output (Ctrl+C to stop following) docker logs -t web # prefix each line with a timestamp Logs survive the crash A container that died still has its logs until you remove it. That is why the first step with a container that \"won't start\" is never to run it again, but to read what the last attempt said: docker ps -a , then docker logs NAME ."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"docker-exec-look-from-the-inside","h":"docker exec: look from the inside","x":"docker exec: look from the inside docker exec starts an additional process inside a running container. It shares the container's file system, network and environment, so it sees exactly what the application sees. docker exec web env # the environment the app was given docker exec web ls /app # files in the image docker exec -it web sh # an interactive shell; leave with: exit -i keeps standard input open and -t allocates a terminal; together they give you an interactive session. Many small images contain sh but not bash : $ docker exec -it web bash OCI runtime exec failed: exec failed: unable to start container process: exec: \"bash\": executable file not found in $PATH: unknown That message does not mean the container is broken. It means the image has no bash . Use sh . exec needs a running container You can't exec into a container that has exited: there is nothing to attach to. For a container that dies at startup the evidence is in docker logs and docker inspect , not in a shell."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"docker-inspect-how-was-it-configured","h":"docker inspect: how was it configured?","x":"docker inspect: how was it configured? docker inspect prints everything Docker knows about an object as JSON: the image, the command, environment variables, mounts, port mappings, network addresses, the state and the exit code. The output is long, so pick out what you need with a Go template: docker inspect web # everything docker inspect -f '{{.State.Status}} {{.State.ExitCode}}' web docker inspect -f '{{json .Config.Env}}' web # environment variables docker inspect -f '{{json .Mounts}}' web # volumes and bind mounts docker inspect -f '{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.IPAddress}}{{end}}' web What you can and cannot change afterwards A container's image, command, environment variables, port mappings and mounts are fixed when it is created . docker restart re-runs the same configuration. To change any of them you remove the container and run a new one. This is by design: containers are meant to be replaced, not repaired."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"scenario-1-explore-an-unknown-container","h":"Scenario 1: explore an unknown container","x":"Scenario 1: explore an unknown container Use the three commands to find out what a running container is doing. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"scenario-2-the-container-that-exits-immediately","h":"Scenario 2: the container that exits immediately","x":"Scenario 2: the container that exits immediately Now the toolkit pays off. Something was started, Docker printed an ID, and it is not running. Find out why before you fix it. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A printed container ID means \"created and started\", not \"working\". Always check docker ps . docker ps -a shows exited containers and their exit code. docker logs shows why. docker exec runs an extra process inside a running container. Try sh when bash is missing. docker inspect shows the configuration a container was created with. Configuration is fixed at creation. To change it, remove the container and run a new one."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A container shows Exited (1) 10 seconds ago . What is the best next command? A. docker restart NAME B. docker exec -it NAME sh C. docker logs NAME D. docker rmi IMAGE Exit code 1 means the application reported an error, and it wrote the reason to its output. exec is impossible on an exited container, and a restart would repeat the same failure. You started a container without the environment variable it needs. How do you add it? A. Remove the container and run a new one with -e B. docker exec NAME export VAR=value C. docker restart NAME -e VAR=value D. Edit the image The environment is part of the configuration fixed at creation. An export inside an exec session only affects that one extra process. docker exec -it api bash fails with executable file not found in $PATH . What does that tell you? A. The container has crashed B. Docker is misconfigured C. You need to add sudo D. The image does not contain bash; try sh Small images (Alpine-based ones in particular) ship without bash. The container itself is fine."},{"l":2,"s":"02-inside-a-container","t":"Inside a Container: Logs, Exec, Inspect","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading docker container logs docker container exec docker inspect The official postgres image: environment variables"},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies The same image runs in development, in testing and in production. What differs is how you start it: which ports you publish, which settings you pass in, and what should happen when the process dies. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Configure a container from the outside: publish ports correctly, pass environment variables , and choose a restart policy . Why it matters: \"it's running but I can't reach it\" is the single most common Docker complaint, and it is nearly always a port mapping. Knowing how to read the mapping turns a mystery into a ten-second check."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Read and write -p HOST:CONTAINER mappings and explain each side. Tell \"connection refused\" from \"connection reset\" and say what each implies. Configure an application through environment variables. Pick a restart policy and recognise a restart loop. Resolve a host port conflict."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The shop's front end is published as the image shop/web . Its documentation is two lines long: \"Listens on port 8080. Optional environment variables: PORT , API_URL , BANNER .\" That is all you need, if you know how to use it."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"publishing-ports","h":"Publishing ports","x":"Publishing ports docker run -d --name web -p 8080:8080 shop/web:1.0 # │ └─ container port: where the process listens # └─ host port: where you connect from outside The two numbers are independent. The container port is decided by the application (and its configuration); you cannot choose it with -p . The host port is yours to choose, as long as it is free. Mapping Meaning -p 8080:8080 Host 8080 → container 8080 -p 80:8080 Host 80 → container 8080 (users type no port; the app is unchanged) -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 Only reachable from this machine, not from the network -p 8080 Container 8080 on a random free host port (see docker port NAME ) EXPOSE is documentation An image can declare ports with EXPOSE . That shows up in docker ps as 8080/tcp (no arrow), and it tells you where the app is expected to listen. It does not publish anything. Only -p does. Reading the failure When a published port does not answer, the error message already narrows down the cause: What curl says What it means Where to look (7) Failed to connect … Could not connect to server Nothing is listening on that host port: no container publishes it, or the container is not running. docker ps -a (56) Recv failure: Connection reset by peer The host port is published, but nothing listens on the container port it forwards to. Right-hand side of -p versus the port in docker logs An HTTP error page (404, 500, 502) You reached a process. The problem is in the application or behind it. docker logs"},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"environment-variables","h":"Environment variables","x":"Environment variables Environment variables are the standard way to configure a containerised application, because they let one image serve every environment. docker run -d --name web-sale -p 8081:8080 \\ -e BANNER=\"Sale today\" \\ -e API_URL=http://api:3000 \\ shop/web:1.0 docker exec web-sale env # check what the process actually received ❌ Easy to get wrong A misspelt name ( -e BANER=… ) is not an error. The app just never sees its setting. -e placed after the image name is passed to the application as an argument, not to Docker. Secrets passed with -e are visible to anyone who can run docker inspect . ✅ Habits that help Verify with docker exec NAME env or docker inspect -f '{{json .Config.Env}}' NAME . Options go between docker run and the image: docker run [OPTIONS] IMAGE [COMMAND] . Use --env-file for many variables, and a secrets mechanism for real credentials."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"restart-policies","h":"Restart policies","x":"Restart policies By default a container that exits stays exited. A restart policy tells the Docker daemon to start it again. Policy Behaviour Typical use no (default) Never restart. One-off commands, experiments on-failure[:N] Restart when the exit code is not 0, at most N times. Jobs that may fail transiently always Always restart, also after the Docker daemon or the machine restarts. Long-running services unless-stopped Like always , but a container you stopped by hand stays stopped after a daemon restart. Long-running services docker run -d --name api --restart unless-stopped shop/api:1.0 docker inspect -f '{{.RestartCount}}' api # how often Docker has restarted it A restart policy hides crashes With --restart always , a container that crashes at startup shows Restarting (1) 3 seconds ago in docker ps , then briefly Up , then Restarting again. Docker waits longer between attempts each time. The container is not \"flaky\": it is failing every single time, and docker logs shows the same error repeated once per attempt."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"scenario-1-one-image-two-configurations","h":"Scenario 1: one image, two configurations","x":"Scenario 1: one image, two configurations This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"scenario-2-it-s-running-but-the-page-won-t-load","h":"Scenario 2: \"it's running, but the page won't load\"","x":"Scenario 2: \"it's running, but the page won't load\" Use the table from Reading the failure : the kind of error tells you which side of the mapping to check. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"scenario-3-port-is-already-allocated","h":"Scenario 3: port is already allocated","x":"Scenario 3: port is already allocated This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways -p HOST:CONTAINER : you choose the host port; the application decides the container port. Connection refused : nothing on the host port. Connection reset : published, but nothing listening inside on the mapped port. Environment variables configure the app; a misspelt variable fails silently, so verify with docker exec NAME env . Restart policies keep services up and also disguise crash loops. Check RestartCount and the logs. A host port can be bound once. A docker run that fails on the port leaves a Created container behind."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz An app listens on port 3000 inside its container. You want users to reach it on port 80 of the host. Which option? A. -p 3000:80 B. -p 80:3000 C. -p 80:80 D. -e PORT=80 HOST:CONTAINER. Host 80 forwards to container 3000. -p 3000:80 would forward host 3000 to a container port where nothing listens. curl localhost:8080 answers Recv failure: Connection reset by peer , and docker ps shows 0.0.0.0:8080->80/tcp . What is the most likely cause? A. The app does not listen on port 80 inside the container B. Port 8080 on the host is used by another program C. The container has exited D. The image is corrupt The host side works (the connection was accepted), so the container side of the mapping is wrong. Check the app's log for the port it listens on. A container with --restart always crashes one second after every start. What will docker ps show most of the time? A. Nothing, the container is removed B. Exited (1) C. Up 3 hours D. Restarting (1) … , with growing pauses between attempts Docker keeps retrying with an increasing delay. The fix is in the logs, not in the restart policy."},{"l":3,"s":"03-ports-env-restarts","t":"Ports, Environment Variables and Restart Policies","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Port publishing and mapping docker run: setting environment variables Start containers automatically (restart policies)"},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache So far you have run other people's images. Now you package your own application, learn why the order of lines in a Dockerfile matters, and debug a build that fails and an image that builds but will not run. ⏱ 45 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Write a Dockerfile , understand layers and the build cache , and tell build-time failures from run-time failures. Why it matters: when a deployment fails with Cannot find module or executable file not found , the cause is in the Dockerfile. You can only find it if you know what each instruction did to the image."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain what each common Dockerfile instruction does. Order instructions so that rebuilds are fast. Read BuildKit's output, including a failed step. Distinguish an error in the build from an error when the container starts. Describe what a multi-stage build is for."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation You wrote a small web service. It runs on your laptop with node server.js . To ship it, the team needs an image. A Dockerfile is the recipe that turns your source directory into one."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"a-good-dockerfile","h":"A good Dockerfile","x":"A good Dockerfile FROM node:24-alpine WORKDIR /app COPY package*.json ./ RUN npm ci --omit=dev COPY . . EXPOSE 3000 CMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"] Instruction What it does When it takes effect FROM Starts from an existing image (the base ). Build WORKDIR Sets (and creates) the directory later instructions and the container work in. Build and run COPY Copies files from the build context (the directory you pass to docker build ) into the image. Build RUN Executes a command during the build and saves the result as a layer. Build ENV Sets an environment variable for later build steps and for containers. Build and run EXPOSE Documents the port the app listens on. Publishes nothing. Metadata CMD The default command a container runs. Not executed during the build. Run RUN versus CMD RUN happens once, while the image is built. CMD happens every time a container starts. A typo in a RUN line breaks the build. A typo in CMD builds a perfectly \"successful\" image that crashes the moment you run it."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"building","h":"Building","x":"Building docker build -t hello:1.0 . # │ └─ build context: the directory whose files COPY can see # └─ name:tag for the resulting image [+] Building 3.2s (10/10) FINISHED docker:default => [internal] load build definition from Dockerfile 0.0s => [internal] load metadata for docker.io/library/node:24-alpine 0.6s => [internal] load build context 0.0s => [1/5] FROM docker.io/library/node:24-alpine@sha256:90712d2c6438… 0.0s => [2/5] WORKDIR /app 0.0s => [3/5] COPY package*.json ./ 0.0s => [4/5] RUN npm ci --omit=dev 2.4s => [5/5] COPY . . 0.0s => exporting to image 0.1s => => naming to docker.io/library/hello:1.0 0.0s"},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"layers-and-the-build-cache","h":"Layers and the build cache","x":"Layers and the build cache Every instruction that changes the file system produces a layer . Docker remembers each layer together with the instruction and the inputs that produced it. On the next build, a step whose instruction and inputs are unchanged is not executed again: its layer is taken from the cache , and the output says CACHED . The catch: once one step has to run, every step after it runs too . So the order of instructions decides how much work a rebuild repeats. ❌ Slow: every code change reinstalls everything FROM node:24-alpine WORKDIR /app COPY . . RUN npm ci --omit=dev CMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"] Editing server.js changes the input of COPY . . , so that layer and the slow npm ci after it are rebuilt. ✅ Fast: dependencies stay cached FROM node:24-alpine WORKDIR /app COPY package*.json ./ RUN npm ci --omit=dev COPY . . CMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"] The package files rarely change, so npm ci stays cached. Only the last COPY is redone. The rule of thumb: what changes least goes first, what changes most goes last ."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"when-the-build-fails","h":"When the build fails","x":"When the build fails BuildKit marks the failing step with ERROR , prints that step's output, and then shows the Dockerfile with >>> in front of the line: => ERROR [4/4] RUN npm ci --omit=dev 1.2s ------ > [4/4] RUN npm ci --omit=dev: 0.216 npm error enoent Could not read package.json: Error: ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '/app/package.json' ------ Dockerfile:4 -------------------- 3 | COPY server.js . 4 | >>> RUN npm ci --omit=dev 5 | EXPOSE 3000 -------------------- ERROR: failed to build: failed to solve: process \"/bin/sh -c npm ci --omit=dev\" did not complete successfully: exit code: 254 Read it bottom-up: which line, which command, and then the command's own message. Here npm cannot find package.json inside the image , because no earlier instruction copied it there. Symptom Typical cause failed to compute cache key: \"/x\": not found COPY names a file that is not in the build context (wrong path, or excluded by .dockerignore ). /bin/sh: apt-get: not found Wrong package manager for the base image. Alpine uses apk , Debian and Ubuntu use apt-get . pull access denied … repository does not exist on FROM A typo in the base image name or tag. Build succeeds; container exits with Cannot find module CMD points at a file that is not where it says, or dependencies were never installed."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"smaller-images-multi-stage-builds","h":"Smaller images: multi-stage builds","x":"Smaller images: multi-stage builds Build tools are needed to produce the application but not to run it. A multi-stage Dockerfile uses one stage to build and a second, clean stage that copies only the result: FROM node:24-alpine AS build WORKDIR /app COPY package*.json ./ RUN npm ci COPY . . RUN npm run build FROM nginx:1.29-alpine COPY --from=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html The final image contains nginx and the built files, and nothing of Node.js, node_modules or your source code. Smaller images pull faster and have fewer packages that can contain vulnerabilities. Two more habits Add a .dockerignore file listing node_modules , .git and local secrets, so they are neither sent to the builder nor copied by COPY . . . Prefer the JSON form CMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"] . The plain form CMD node server.js wraps your process in a shell, which then receives the stop signal instead of your application."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"scenario-1-build-your-own-image","h":"Scenario 1: build your own image","x":"Scenario 1: build your own image This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"scenario-2-the-build-that-fails-then-the-image-that-crashes","h":"Scenario 2: the build that fails, then the image that crashes","x":"Scenario 2: the build that fails, then the image that crashes Two mistakes, two different places to find the evidence: the build output for one, docker logs for the other. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A Dockerfile is a list of instructions; file-changing instructions create layers. RUN executes at build time, CMD at container start. Each fails in a different place. A changed step invalidates the cache for all later steps: copy dependency manifests and install before copying the source. Build errors: read the >>> line and the step output above it. Run-time errors: docker logs . Images are immutable. After changing the Dockerfile, rebuild and create a new container."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz You change one line in server.js and rebuild the \"fast\" Dockerfile from this lesson. Which steps are executed again? A. All of them B. RUN npm ci and everything after it C. Only COPY . . (and the export) D. None; the image is identical The package files did not change, so the steps up to and including npm ci are cached. COPY . . has new input and is redone. A build ends with /bin/sh: apt-get: not found on a RUN apt-get install -y curl line. The base image is node:24-alpine . What is wrong? A. Alpine uses apk , not apt-get B. The network is down C. curl does not exist for Node D. RUN needs sudo The command itself is missing from the base image. On Alpine the equivalent is RUN apk add --no-cache curl . docker build succeeds, but docker run exits at once with Error: Cannot find module '/app/app.js' . Where is the mistake most likely? A. In a RUN instruction B. In CMD (or in which files were copied to where) C. In EXPOSE D. In the build cache A successful build proves the instructions ran, not that CMD names a file that exists in the image. The container's log names the missing file."},{"l":4,"s":"04-building-images","t":"Building Images: Dockerfile, Layers and Cache","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Dockerfile reference Docker build cache Multi-stage builds Building best practices"},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container Containers are meant to be thrown away and replaced. Databases are not. This lesson explains where a container's data really lives and how to keep the part that matters. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand the container's writable layer , and use named volumes and bind mounts to store data outside it. Why it matters: \"we redeployed and the data is gone\" is one of the few container mistakes that cannot be undone. It is easy to avoid once you know which path must be on a volume."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain what happens to files written inside a container. Create, mount, inspect and remove named volumes. Choose between a named volume and a bind mount. Recognise an anonymous volume and explain where it came from. Find out why data disappeared when a container was replaced."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The shop stores its orders in PostgreSQL, running in a container. Every deployment replaces that container with a new one. The orders have to still be there afterwards."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"where-files-go-by-default","h":"Where files go by default","x":"Where files go by default An image is a stack of read-only layers. When a container is created, Docker adds one thin writable layer on top. Every file the container creates or changes ends up there. ┌──────────────────────────────┐ │ writable layer (this container only) ← deleted by docker rm ├──────────────────────────────┤ │ image layer: application code ┐ │ image layer: dependencies │ read-only, shared by all │ image layer: base operating system ┘ containers of this image └──────────────────────────────┘ Action Writable layer docker stop / docker start / docker restart Kept. It is the same container. docker rm , then docker run again Gone. The new container starts from the image. Deploying a new image version Gone. A new version always means a new container."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"named-volumes","h":"Named volumes","x":"Named volumes A volume is storage that Docker manages separately from any container. You mount it at a path; whatever the container writes below that path goes to the volume. The volume has its own lifetime and survives the container. docker volume create dbdata # optional: run creates it on first use docker run -d --name db \\ -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret \\ -v dbdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data \\ postgres:17 # │ └─ path inside the container # └─ volume name docker volume ls docker volume inspect dbdata Remove the container, run a new one with the same -v , and the database finds its files again. Its log even says so: PostgreSQL Database directory appears to contain a database; Skipping initialization The path must be the one the application writes to A volume protects exactly the directory it is mounted on, and nothing else. Mounting dbdata at /data does nothing for a database that keeps its files in /var/lib/postgresql/data . The correct path is part of each image's documentation. An initialised volume ignores new settings The PostgreSQL image reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD , POSTGRES_USER and POSTGRES_DB only when it initialises an empty data directory. Start it later with a different password on the same volume and the old password still applies. This surprises people regularly: \"I changed the password in the config and it has no effect.\""},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"bind-mounts","h":"Bind mounts","x":"Bind mounts A bind mount maps a directory or file of the host into the container. Docker recognises it by the path on the left-hand side: docker run -d --name site -p 8080:80 \\ -v ./public:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro \\ nginx:1.29 # │ └─ read-only inside the container # └─ starts with ./ or / : a host path, not a volume name Named volume Bind mount Written as -v name:/path -v /host/path:/path or -v ./dir:/path Stored In Docker's own storage area Wherever the host path points Best for Application data: databases, uploads Injecting configuration files; live source code during development Watch out for Easy to forget it exists ( docker volume ls ) Ties the container to one machine's directory layout and permissions"},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"anonymous-volumes","h":"Anonymous volumes","x":"Anonymous volumes An image can declare a path as a volume in its Dockerfile ( VOLUME /var/lib/postgresql/data ). If you start a container without mounting anything there, Docker silently creates an anonymous volume for it, named with a long random ID: DRIVER VOLUME NAME local dbdata local f116cb4717682c4bd02be4da511fb48186c2b233e00fe79b2819bd1950e4245c The data in it survives stop and start , which makes everything look fine. But a new container gets a new anonymous volume, so the old data is orphaned the moment you replace the container. This is the mechanism behind most \"we lost the data\" stories. docker inspect -f '{{json .Mounts}}' db # what is mounted where, and from which volume docker volume prune # delete anonymous volumes no container uses Commands that delete data docker volume rm NAME , docker volume prune , docker rm -v and, in the next lessons, docker compose down -v delete volumes for good. There is no undo. Removing a container never deletes a named volume."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"scenario-1-data-that-survives","h":"Scenario 1: data that survives","x":"Scenario 1: data that survives This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"scenario-2-the-orders-vanish-after-every-deploy","h":"Scenario 2: the orders vanish after every deploy","x":"Scenario 2: the orders vanish after every deploy The team says they use a volume, and docker volume ls agrees. Look at where it is mounted. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Files written in a container go to its writable layer, which is deleted with the container. A named volume has its own lifetime. Mount it at the exact directory the application stores data in. Bind mounts map host paths and are best for configuration and development. Images that declare VOLUME get an anonymous volume if you mount nothing: data survives restarts but not replacement. docker inspect -f '{{json .Mounts}}' shows the truth about what is stored where."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A container wrote a file to /tmp/report.txt (no volume there). Which action loses the file? A. docker stop followed by docker start B. docker rm followed by a new docker run C. docker restart D. Running docker exec in it Stop, start and restart keep the same container and its writable layer. Removing the container deletes that layer. What does -v ./conf:/etc/app create? A. A named volume called ./conf B. An anonymous volume C. A copy of the directory inside the image D. A bind mount of the host directory ./conf A left-hand side that starts with / or ./ is a host path. A plain name would be a named volume. PostgreSQL runs with -v dbdata:/data . After replacing the container the tables are empty. Why? A. The database writes to /var/lib/postgresql/data , which was not on the named volume B. Named volumes are emptied when a container is removed C. The volume was mounted read-only D. PostgreSQL does not support volumes The named volume was mounted on a path the database never uses. The real data went to an anonymous volume that the new container does not share."},{"l":5,"s":"05-volumes","t":"Volumes: Data That Survives the Container","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Volumes Bind mounts Storage overview"},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Container Networking and DNS Real applications are several containers that talk to each other. This lesson shows how containers find each other by name, why that sometimes fails, and how to tell a naming problem from a connection problem. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Connect containers with user-defined networks , understand Docker's built-in DNS , and classify network errors by their message. Why it matters: network errors look alike to beginners and are completely different to someone who can read them. \"Not found\", \"refused\" and \"timed out\" each point to a different layer, here and later in Kubernetes."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain the difference between the default bridge network and a user-defined network. Make one container reach another by name. Explain when -p is needed and when it is not. Map an error message to \"name resolution\", \"nothing listening\" or \"blocked\". Inspect networks and attach running containers to them."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The shop has three parts: a web front end, an API, and a PostgreSQL database. The API has to reach the database; the front end has to reach the API; customers only ever reach the front end."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"every-container-has-its-own-network-stack","h":"Every container has its own network stack","x":"Every container has its own network stack Each container gets its own network interface and IP address. localhost inside a container is that container , not the host and not its neighbours. The localhost trap An API configured with DATABASE_URL=postgres://…@localhost:5432/… works on a laptop where everything runs side by side, and fails in containers with connection refused : there is no database inside the API's own container. Between containers you connect to the other container's name ."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"the-default-bridge-versus-your-own-network","h":"The default bridge versus your own network","x":"The default bridge versus your own network Without --network , a container joins the built-in network called bridge . Containers there can reach each other by IP address, but there is no name resolution . On a network you create yourself, Docker runs a small DNS server, and every container is reachable by its name. Default bridge User-defined network Reach another container by IP Yes Yes Reach another container by name No Yes Isolation from other applications Shared by everything started without --network Only the containers you attach docker network create shopnet docker run -d --name db --network shopnet \\ -e POSTGRES_USER=shop -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret postgres:17 docker run -d --name api --network shopnet -p 3000:3000 \\ -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://shop:secret@db:5432/shop shop/api:1.0 # └─ the database container's name curl localhost:3000/products"},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"when-do-you-need-p","h":"When do you need -p?","x":"When do you need -p? Publishing is for traffic that comes from outside Docker: your browser, curl on the host, other machines. Containers on the same network reach each other's ports directly, on the port the application listens on. your shell ──► localhost:3000 ──(-p 3000:3000)──► api:3000 ──► db:5432 published on the host container to container on shopnet, no -p needed Publish as little as possible The database above has no -p at all, so nothing outside the Docker network can connect to it. That is a deliberate security benefit, not an omission. Publishing 5432 \"to make the API work\" would not even help: the API does not go through the host."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"reading-network-errors","h":"Reading network errors","x":"Reading network errors This table is worth memorising. It applies to Docker, to Kubernetes and to networking in general. Message contains What happened Check ENOTFOUND , bad address , Could not resolve host , NXDOMAIN Name resolution failed. No connection was even attempted. Spelling of the name; are both containers on the same user-defined network? ECONNREFUSED , Connection refused The name resolved and the machine answered, but nothing listens on that port . Is the target running? Is it listening on that port? Did it finish starting? ETIMEDOUT , timed out The packets went nowhere: dropped by a firewall or policy , or the address is unreachable. Firewalls, network policies, wrong IP authentication failed , HTTP 401/403 The network is fine. The credentials are wrong. User, password, token"},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"looking-at-networks","h":"Looking at networks","x":"Looking at networks docker network ls docker network inspect shopnet # subnet, gateway and the containers attached docker inspect -f '{{json .NetworkSettings.Networks}}' api # test from a throw-away container on the same network docker run --rm --network shopnet alpine nslookup db docker run --rm --network shopnet alpine wget -qO- http://api:3000/healthz docker run --rm --network shopnet alpine nc -zv db 5432 A running container can be attached to an additional network without restarting it: docker network connect shopnet api docker network disconnect bridge api"},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"scenario-1-connect-containers-by-name","h":"Scenario 1: connect containers by name","x":"Scenario 1: connect containers by name This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"scenario-2-getaddrinfo-enotfound-db","h":"Scenario 2: getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND db","x":"Scenario 2: getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND db Both containers are up. Classify the error first, then look at the networks. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways localhost inside a container is the container itself. Name resolution between containers exists only on user-defined networks. The default bridge has none. -p is for access from outside Docker. Containers on one network talk directly. Not found = name. Refused = no listener. Timed out = blocked. Auth failed = credentials. docker network inspect shows who is attached; docker network connect fixes it without a restart."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Two containers were started with plain docker run (no --network ). Container A tries to reach http://b:8080 . What happens? A. It works, because both are on the bridge network B. Connection refused C. The name b cannot be resolved D. The request reaches the host instead The default bridge network has no DNS between containers. The fix is a user-defined network. The API logs connect ECONNREFUSED 172.18.0.2:5432 . What does this tell you? A. The name resolved, but nothing is listening on that address and port (yet) B. The database's name is misspelt C. The password is wrong D. A firewall dropped the packets An IP address in the message proves that resolution worked. \"Refused\" means the port is closed: the database is not running, still starting, or listening elsewhere. The database container has no -p option. Which statement is true? A. No container can connect to it B. Containers on its network can connect to it; the host and other machines cannot C. It listens on a random port D. It must be restarted with -p 5432:5432 before the API can use it Publishing only concerns access from outside Docker. Keeping internal services unpublished is the safer default."},{"l":6,"s":"06-networking","t":"Container Networking and DNS","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Networking overview Bridge network driver: user-defined versus default bridge docker network connect"},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File Three containers, a network, a volume, five environment variables: typing that as docker run commands every time is slow and error-prone. Compose describes the whole application in one file that you can read, review and commit. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Describe a multi-container application in a Compose file , operate it with docker compose , and debug a stack that \"started\" but does not work. Why it matters: Compose is how most teams run their application locally and in CI. It is also your first contact with declarative configuration, the idea Kubernetes is built on."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Read and write a Compose file with services, ports, environment, volumes and dependencies. Start, inspect, update and remove a stack. Explain how services find each other. Say what depends_on guarantees and what it does not. Debug a stack one service at a time."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation In Lesson 6 you started the database and the API by hand. A new colleague needs the same setup on their machine, and next month there will be five services. The commands have to become a file."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"the-compose-file","h":"The Compose file","x":"The Compose file services: db: image: postgres:17 environment: POSTGRES_USER: shop POSTGRES_PASSWORD: secret volumes: - dbdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data api: image: shop/api:1.0 environment: DATABASE_URL: postgres://shop:secret@db:5432/shop ports: - \"3000:3000\" depends_on: - db volumes: dbdata: Every key maps to something you already know: Compose docker run equivalent Service name ( db , api ) --name , and the DNS name on the project's network image: The image argument environment: -e ports: -p (quote the values: \"3000:3000\" ) volumes: under a service -v Top-level volumes: docker volume create build: . docker build for that service The network comes for free Compose creates one user-defined network per project and attaches every service to it. That is why the API can use the host name db : the service name is the DNS name . Nearly every \"service A can't find service B\" problem in Compose is a host name that does not match a service name."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"operating-a-stack","h":"Operating a stack","x":"Operating a stack docker compose up -d # create and start everything, in the background docker compose ps # containers of this project (add -a for stopped ones) docker compose logs api # one service's output; add -f to follow docker compose exec db psql -U shop -c \"SELECT 1\" docker compose down # remove containers and the network; keep volumes docker compose down -v # ...and delete the volumes: your data is gone [+] Running 4/4 ✔ Network shop_default Created 0.1s ✔ Volume \"shop_dbdata\" Created 0.2s ✔ Container shop-db-1 Started 0.3s ✔ Container shop-api-1 Started 0.4s Names are prefixed with the project name , which defaults to the name of the directory containing the file. The same file in two directories gives two independent stacks. up is declarative docker compose up -d does not mean \"start\". It means \"make reality match this file\". Run it again after editing the file and Compose changes only what differs: a new service is created, a service whose configuration changed is recreated, everything else is left running. \"Started\" is not \"working\" Compose reports Started as soon as the container's process has been launched. A database that exits one second later because of a missing password was still \"Started\". After up , always look at docker compose ps -a : a healthy stack shows every service as Up ."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"depends-on-and-what-it-does-not-promise","h":"depends_on, and what it does not promise","x":"depends_on, and what it does not promise depends_on controls start order : Compose starts db before api . It does not wait until the database is ready to accept connections, which can take several seconds. An application that connects immediately and gives up on the first failure will crash on a cold start. There are two proper remedies. Make the application retry its connection, which it needs anyway for restarts in production. Or give the dependency a healthcheck and require condition: service_healthy : services: db: image: postgres:17 healthcheck: test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U shop\"] interval: 5s retries: 10 api: depends_on: db: condition: service_healthy"},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"debugging-a-stack-outside-in","h":"Debugging a stack: outside in","x":"Debugging a stack: outside in When the application as a whole is broken, follow a request from the outside inwards, and at each hop ask the same three questions. Is it running? docker compose ps -a . Anything Exited or Restarting ? What does it say? docker compose logs SERVICE . Can I reach it, and can it reach the next hop? curl the published port; read the error class (Lesson 6). curl localhost:8080 → \"Products: unavailable (api responded 500 …)\" web is fine; look at api curl localhost:3000/products → \"getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND database\" api can't resolve its db host compose.yaml → DATABASE_URL uses \"database\", the service is \"db\" Validate before you start docker compose config parses the file and prints the result with all defaults filled in, or an error with the offending key. It catches indentation mistakes and references to undefined volumes or services before any container is touched."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"scenario-1-the-whole-shop-in-one-file","h":"Scenario 1: the whole shop in one file","x":"Scenario 1: the whole shop in one file This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"scenario-2-three-typos-one-broken-stack","h":"Scenario 2: three typos, one broken stack","x":"Scenario 2: three typos, one broken stack Everything \"Started\". Nothing works. Three independent mistakes, each with its own signature. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A Compose file declares services, their configuration, volumes and dependencies. Each project gets its own network; service names are DNS names. up -d reconciles reality with the file and only changes what differs. \"Started\" only means launched. Check docker compose ps -a and the logs. depends_on orders startup; it does not wait for readiness unless you add a health check. down keeps named volumes, down -v deletes them."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz In a Compose project, service web must call service api on port 3000. Which URL does web use? A. http://localhost:3000 B. http://api:3000 C. http://shop-api-1.local:3000 D. The host's IP address and a published port Services share the project network, where each service name resolves to its container. localhost would be the web container itself. You change one service's environment in compose.yaml and run docker compose up -d again. What happens? A. Nothing; you must run down first B. All containers are recreated C. Only the changed service's container is recreated D. Compose reports an error because the stack is already up up compares the desired configuration with the running containers and recreates only those that differ. api has depends_on: [db] and crashes on a cold start with \"connection refused\", but works when started a few seconds later. Why? A. depends_on only orders startup; the database process was not ready yet B. The service name is wrong C. The database port must be published D. Compose started the services in the wrong order Start order is not readiness. Use a health check with condition: service_healthy , and make the app retry."},{"l":7,"s":"07-compose","t":"Docker Compose: A Whole Stack in One File","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Docker Compose documentation Compose file reference Control startup order Networking in Compose"},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method You have now met most of the ways a container can fail, one at a time. Real incidents rarely come one at a time. This lesson turns what you learned into a method you can apply when you don't yet know what is wrong. ⏱ 45 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Apply a repeatable four-step method to any misbehaving container, and solve an incident with several simultaneous faults. Why it matters: under pressure people restart things, change three settings at once and lose track of what helped. A method keeps you calm and fast, and it is the same method you will use on Kubernetes."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Work through state, logs, configuration and connectivity in order. Interpret exit codes, including 137 and OOM kills. Set and check memory limits. Untangle several faults by fixing one visible error at a time. Clean up a Docker host safely."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"the-method-four-questions-in-this-order","h":"The method: four questions, in this order","x":"The method: four questions, in this order # Question Command You learn 1 State : is it running? Since when? How did it end? docker ps -a Up / Exited (code) / Restarting / Created; ports; age 2 Logs : what did the process say? docker logs --tail 50 NAME The application's own error message 3 Configuration : how was it started? docker inspect NAME Image and tag, env, ports, mounts, networks, limits, restart count 4 Connectivity : can it be reached, and can it reach what it needs? curl , docker exec … wget/nc/nslookup , docker network inspect Error class: not found / refused / reset / timed out / auth The order matters. There is no point testing connectivity to a container that is not running, and no point reading configuration before the logs have told you what to look for. Three rules that save hours Read before you change. Collect the state and the message first. A restart destroys evidence and usually reproduces the failure. One change at a time. Then test again. If you change three things and it works, you learned nothing; if it doesn't, you may have added a fault. Fix the error you can see. When several things are broken, each fix reveals the next message. That is progress, not failure."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"what-the-state-tells-you","h":"What the state tells you","x":"What the state tells you STATUS Meaning Next step Up 3 hours The process is alive. If it misbehaves, the problem is inside it or around it. Logs, then connectivity Exited (0) Finished normally. Wrong for a server: was the right command started? Logs; inspect the command Exited (1) The application failed. Logs Exited (127) The command does not exist in the image. inspect : Path, Args, image tag Exited (137) Killed: by docker kill , a stop timeout, or the OOM killer. inspect -f '{{.State.OOMKilled}}' Restarting (N) … Crash loop under a restart policy. Logs (the same error repeats) Created Never started, typically because docker run failed on a port or network. No logs exist. Free the resource, docker start"},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"memory-limits-and-exit-code-137","h":"Memory limits and exit code 137","x":"Memory limits and exit code 137 A container can use as much of the host's memory as it likes unless you limit it. A limit is enforced by the kernel: when the container's processes need more than the limit, the kernel's out-of-memory killer ends one with SIGKILL. The process cannot catch that signal and logs nothing. docker run -d --name api -m 256m shop/api:1.0 # hard limit: 256 MiB docker stats --no-stream # current usage versus limit docker inspect -f '{{.State.ExitCode}} {{.State.OOMKilled}}' api CONTAINER ID NAME CPU % MEM USAGE / LIMIT MEM % NET I/O BLOCK I/O PIDS ee8fe5ec7647 api 0.60% 125.8MiB / 256MiB 49.14% 1.2kB / 0B 0B / 0B 11 ❌ Misreading 137 \"Exit code 137 and nothing in the logs: the app is silently broken.\" Then an afternoon is spent in the application's code. ✅ Reading it correctly 137 = 128 + 9 = killed from outside. Check OOMKilled . If it is true, compare the limit with real usage ( docker stats ) and raise the limit or reduce the usage."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"keeping-a-host-tidy","h":"Keeping a host tidy","x":"Keeping a host tidy Stopped containers, unused images and forgotten volumes pile up and eventually fill the disk, which causes its own strange failures. docker system df # what uses space: images, containers, volumes, build cache docker container prune # remove all stopped containers docker image prune # remove dangling images (add -a for all unused ones) docker system prune # containers + networks + dangling images + build cache Prune with care docker system prune never deletes named volumes unless you add --volumes . With that flag it deletes every volume no container currently uses, including the database volume of a stack you merely stopped. Read the confirmation prompt."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"scenario-1-exit-code-137","h":"Scenario 1: exit code 137","x":"Scenario 1: exit code 137 Start with state and logs. Notice what the logs do not contain. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"scenario-2-monday-morning-the-shop-is-down","h":"Scenario 2: Monday morning, the shop is down","x":"Scenario 2: Monday morning, the shop is down This is the Docker capstone: four containers, several faults, no description of what was changed. Apply the method to one container at a time, and let each error message lead you to the next step. Use the hints only when you are stuck for more than a few minutes. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"what-changes-in-kubernetes","h":"What changes in Kubernetes","x":"What changes in Kubernetes From the next lesson on, Kubernetes starts the containers for you, on several machines. The four questions stay exactly the same; only the commands change. Question Docker Kubernetes State docker ps -a kubectl get pods Logs docker logs kubectl logs Configuration and events docker inspect kubectl describe , kubectl get -o yaml Connectivity docker exec , curl kubectl exec , kubectl get endpoints"},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways State → logs → configuration → connectivity. Always in that order. Read first, change one thing, test again. Exit codes above 128 mean \"killed by signal\". 137 with OOMKilled: true is a memory limit, not an application bug. A Created container never ran and has no logs; a Restarting one fails the same way on every attempt. With several faults, each fix uncovers the next error. Follow the messages."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A container is Restarting (1) 4 seconds ago . What should you do first? A. docker restart it B. docker logs --tail 50 to read the error it repeats C. Remove its restart policy D. Reboot the host The container fails on every attempt for the same reason, and the reason is in its output. Restarting adds one more identical failure. A container exits with code 137, OOMKilled is true , and its logs end with normal request lines. What is the cause? A. An unhandled exception B. A wrong CMD C. A failed image pull D. The process exceeded the container's memory limit and was killed by the kernel SIGKILL cannot be caught, so nothing is logged. The fix is to size the limit from measured usage, or to reduce the usage. You fixed a crash at startup, and now the app answers with \"password authentication failed\". What does that tell you? A. You made progress: the app starts, reaches the database, and the next fault is the credentials B. Your fix was wrong; undo it C. The network is broken D. The image must be rebuilt A new, later-stage error is evidence that the earlier stages now work. Authentication can only fail after name resolution and the connection succeeded."},{"l":8,"s":"08-troubleshooting-docker","t":"Troubleshooting Docker: A Method","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Resource constraints (memory and CPU limits) docker container stats Prune unused Docker objects"},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"How Kubernetes Works Docker runs containers on one machine. Kubernetes runs them on many, keeps them running, and lets you describe what you want instead of what to do. Before the first YAML file, this lesson gives you the map. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand the parts of a cluster , the idea of desired state , and the basic kubectl commands for looking around. Why it matters: Kubernetes error messages name its components: the scheduler could not place a pod, the kubelet could not pull an image, the API server rejected a field. Knowing who does what tells you where each problem lives."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Name the control-plane and node components and their jobs. Explain \"desired state\" and \"reconciliation\". List, describe and delete objects with kubectl . Explain what a pod is and how it differs from a container. Follow a pod's creation through its events."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The shop outgrew a single Docker host. One machine is a single point of failure, and somebody had to notice and react whenever a container died. The team moved to a three-node Kubernetes cluster, and you have just been given access."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"what-kubernetes-adds","h":"What Kubernetes adds","x":"What Kubernetes adds Need With plain Docker With Kubernetes Run 4 copies across machines You choose machines and start each one You say \"4 replicas\"; the scheduler places them A container dies Restart policy on that one host Recreated automatically, on another node if necessary A machine dies Everything on it is down until you act Its pods are recreated on the remaining nodes Find a service Container names on one network A stable name and virtual IP per Service, cluster-wide Release a new version Stop old, start new, by hand Rolling update with automatic pause on failure, and rollback"},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"the-parts-of-a-cluster","h":"The parts of a cluster","x":"The parts of a cluster ┌──────────────── control plane (cp-1) ────────────────┐ kubectl ──►│ API server ◄──► etcd (the cluster's database) │ │ ▲ ▲ │ │ │ └── controller manager (keeps counts right) │ │ └─────── scheduler (picks a node for each pod) │ └─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ every component talks only to the API server ┌─────────┴──────────┐ ┌────────────────────┐ │ worker-1 │ │ worker-2 │ │ kubelet │ │ kubelet │ │ container runtime │ │ container runtime │ │ kube-proxy │ │ kube-proxy │ │ [pod] [pod] [pod] │ │ [pod] [pod] │ └────────────────────┘ └────────────────────┘ Component Job You notice it when API server The only entry point. Validates and stores every object. A manifest is rejected: error from server… etcd Stores the cluster's state. Rarely, as a user Scheduler Assigns each new pod to a node that fits. A pod stays Pending : FailedScheduling Controller manager Runs the control loops (Deployments, ReplicaSets, Jobs, …). Pods are created, replaced, scaled kubelet On each node: starts and watches the containers of the pods assigned to it, runs probes. ImagePullBackOff , CrashLoopBackOff , probe failures Container runtime Actually runs containers (containerd). Docker is not involved. failed to create containerd task… kube-proxy Makes Service IPs work on each node. Service routing"},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"desired-state-and-reconciliation","h":"Desired state and reconciliation","x":"Desired state and reconciliation This is the idea everything else rests on. You do not tell Kubernetes to do something. You store an object that describes what you want , and controllers work continuously to make reality match. you: \"there should be 3 replicas of web\" (desired state, stored in the API) controller: counts 2 running → creates 1 later: a node dies, 1 pod is gone → counts 2 → creates 1 forever: observe → compare → act The practical consequence: you fix things by changing the declaration , and you never need to \"restart Kubernetes\" to make it notice. It is always looking."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"pods","h":"Pods","x":"Pods The smallest thing Kubernetes runs is a pod : one or more containers that share a network address and can share storage. Most pods contain exactly one container. Think of a pod as \"a container plus the wrapper Kubernetes needs\": an IP address, labels, a restart policy, a place on a node. Pods are disposable A pod is never repaired or moved. When something goes wrong it is replaced by a new pod, with a new name and a new IP address. That is why you rarely create pods directly (the next lesson introduces Deployments) and why nothing should depend on a pod's IP (Lesson 11 introduces Services)."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"kubectl-looking-around","h":"kubectl: looking around","x":"kubectl: looking around kubectl talks to the API server. Nearly every command follows the pattern kubectl VERB TYPE [NAME] [flags] . kubectl get nodes -o wide # the machines kubectl get pods # pods in the current namespace (default) kubectl get pods -A # pods in all namespaces kubectl get pods -o wide # adds the pod's IP and node kubectl describe pod NAME # everything about one pod, with its events kubectl logs NAME # the container's output kubectl delete pod NAME kubectl api-resources # every object type the cluster knows NAME STATUS ROLES AGE VERSION cp-1 Ready control-plane 4d v1.37.0 worker-1 Ready <none> 4d v1.37.0 worker-2 Ready <none> 4d v1.37.0 Namespaces divide a cluster into named areas. The cluster's own components live in kube-system ; your objects go to default unless you say otherwise with -n NAME . Lesson 19 covers them properly."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"reading-a-pod-s-events","h":"Reading a pod's events","x":"Reading a pod's events The bottom of kubectl describe pod is the most useful block of text in Kubernetes. Each line is one component reporting what it did: Events: Type Reason Age From Message ---- ------ ---- ---- ------- Normal Scheduled 12s default-scheduler Successfully assigned default/hello to worker-1 Normal Pulling 12s kubelet Pulling image \"nginx:1.29\" Normal Pulled 10s kubelet Successfully pulled image \"nginx:1.29\" in 2s (2s including waiting). Image size: 192000000 bytes. Normal Created 10s kubelet Created container: hello Normal Started 10s kubelet Started container hello Scheduled → Pulling → Pulled → Created → Started. When a pod is unhealthy, this chain stops somewhere and a Warning line says why. Finding that line is the core of Lessons 15 to 17."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"scenario-a-tour-of-the-cluster","h":"Scenario: a tour of the cluster","x":"Scenario: a tour of the cluster This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Everything goes through the API server; controllers, the scheduler and the kubelets all react to what is stored there. You declare desired state. Controllers reconcile continuously: observe, compare, act. A pod is one or more containers with a shared IP. Pods are replaced, never repaired. kubectl get lists, describe explains (with events), logs shows output. The events of a pod tell the story of its start: Scheduled, Pulling, Pulled, Created, Started."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Which component decides on which node a new pod runs? A. The kubelet B. etcd C. The scheduler D. kube-proxy The scheduler assigns pods to nodes. The kubelet on the chosen node then starts the containers. What does \"reconciliation\" mean in Kubernetes? A. Controllers continuously compare the desired state with the actual state and act on the difference B. Pods are restarted every few minutes C. kubectl retries failed commands D. Nodes synchronise their clocks You store what you want; control loops keep making reality match it, for as long as the object exists. A pod's events end with Scheduled and Pulling , and nothing after that for several minutes. Which component is working on it, and on what? A. The scheduler, choosing a node B. The kubelet on the assigned node, pulling the image C. The API server, validating the manifest D. The controller manager, counting replicas Scheduling is finished (the pod has a node). Pulling images is the kubelet's job, so the delay or failure is there."},{"l":9,"s":"09-how-kubernetes-works","t":"How Kubernetes Works","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Kubernetes components Pods Controllers kubectl quick reference"},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing You almost never create pods by hand. You write a Deployment that says how many copies of which pod you want, and Kubernetes keeps that true. This lesson covers the manifest, the three objects it creates, and the errors the API server reports when the manifest is wrong. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Write and apply a Deployment manifest, understand the chain Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod , and read the API server's validation errors. Why it matters: the Deployment is the object you will edit most often, and a rejected manifest is the first failure every newcomer meets. The messages are precise once you know how to read them."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain every part of a Deployment manifest. Use kubectl apply to create and change objects declaratively. Explain how labels and selectors tie the objects together. Describe what happens when a pod is deleted or a replica count changes. Fix a manifest from the API server's error messages."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The front end has to run as two copies, come back by itself when a copy dies, and be easy to scale for a sale. That description is, almost word for word, a Deployment."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"the-manifest","h":"The manifest","x":"The manifest apiVersion: apps/v1 # API group and version this kind belongs to kind: Deployment metadata: name: web labels: app: web spec: replicas: 2 # how many pods you want selector: matchLabels: app: web # \"my pods are the ones labelled app=web\" template: # the pod to stamp out metadata: labels: app: web # must match the selector above spec: containers: - name: web image: shop/web:1.0 ports: - containerPort: 8080 env: - name: API_URL value: http://api:3000 Every Kubernetes object has the same four top-level parts: apiVersion , kind , metadata (name, labels) and spec (what you want). Kubernetes adds a fifth, status (what is currently true)."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"apply-declare-don-t-command","h":"apply: declare, don't command","x":"apply: declare, don't command kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml # create it, or change it to match the file kubectl get deployments kubectl rollout status deployment/web kubectl get pods deployment.apps/web created NAME READY UP-TO-DATE AVAILABLE AGE web 2/2 2 2 8s kubectl apply is idempotent: run it again with the same file and the answer is unchanged ; edit the file and it says configured . The file is the source of truth. This is the habit to build from day one: ❌ Imperative: quick, and forgotten kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=4 kubectl set image deployment/web web=shop/web:1.1 Works, but the file now lies about what is running. The next apply of the old file silently undoes it. ✅ Declarative: change the file # edit deployment.yaml: replicas: 4 kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml The file, your git history and the cluster agree. Imperative commands are fine for experiments and emergencies."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"deployment-replicaset-pod","h":"Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod","x":"Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod NAME READY UP-TO-DATE AVAILABLE AGE deployment.apps/web 2/2 2 2 40s NAME DESIRED CURRENT READY AGE replicaset.apps/web-j8p7ln5l7t 2 2 2 40s NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE pod/web-j8p7ln5l7t-bbrt5 1/1 Running 0 40s pod/web-j8p7ln5l7t-l4d6g 1/1 Running 0 40s Object Responsibility Deployment Manages versions : creates a new ReplicaSet when the pod template changes and moves pods over (Lesson 13). ReplicaSet Keeps the number of pods for one template version at the desired count. Pod Runs the containers on a node. The names show the ownership: web → web-j8p7ln5l7t (the hash identifies the template version) → web-j8p7ln5l7t-bbrt5 . Labels and selectors Objects are not linked by name but by labels . The ReplicaSet does not keep a list of \"its\" pods; it counts pods whose labels match its selector . Services (next lesson) and network policies find pods the same way. kubectl get pods --show-labels kubectl get pods -l app=web # filter by label Self-healing Delete a pod and the ReplicaSet's count drops below the desired number, so it creates a replacement within a second. The new pod has a new name and a new IP. You did not restart anything; the control loop closed the gap. To stop an application, change the Deployment Deleting pods of a Deployment is futile: they come back. Scale it to zero ( replicas: 0 ) or delete the Deployment, which removes its ReplicaSets and pods with it."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"when-the-api-server-says-no","h":"When the API server says no","x":"When the API server says no A manifest is checked in stages, and each stage has its own style of message. Read the message and you know the stage. Message Stage Usual fix error parsing … yaml: line 12: did not find expected key The file is not valid YAML. Indentation near that line (spaces, never tabs). no matches for kind \"Deployment\" in version \"v1\" kind and apiVersion don't go together. kubectl api-resources shows the right apiVersion ( apps/v1 ). strict decoding error: unknown field \"spec.template.spec.containers[0].imagee\" A field name the API does not know. A typo, or a field at the wrong indentation level. The path is exact. cannot unmarshal string into Go struct field … of type int32 Wrong data type. replicas: \"2\" must be replicas: 2 . The reverse bites with env values: value: \"8080\" must be a string. The Deployment \"web\" is invalid: spec.template.metadata.labels: … selector does not match template labels The object is well-formed but makes no sense. Make the template's labels match spec.selector.matchLabels . Check without changing anything kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f deployment.yaml sends the manifest through all validation stages and stores nothing. kubectl diff -f deployment.yaml shows what an apply would change. kubectl explain deployment.spec.strategy prints the documentation of any field."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"scenario-1-declare-it-and-let-kubernetes-keep-it-running","h":"Scenario 1: declare it, and let Kubernetes keep it running","x":"Scenario 1: declare it, and let Kubernetes keep it running This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"scenario-2-the-manifest-the-api-server-keeps-rejecting","h":"Scenario 2: the manifest the API server keeps rejecting","x":"Scenario 2: the manifest the API server keeps rejecting Three mistakes, reported one at a time. Use the table above to place each message. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A Deployment declares a pod template and a replica count; a ReplicaSet keeps the count; pods run the containers. Objects find each other through labels and selectors, not names. kubectl apply -f makes the cluster match the file. Keep the file as the source of truth. Deleting a pod triggers a replacement. Change the Deployment to change what runs. Validation errors name their stage: YAML syntax, unknown kind, unknown field, wrong type, invalid object."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz You delete one of the three pods of a Deployment. What happens? A. The Deployment now has two pods until you scale it B. The ReplicaSet notices the count is below 3 and creates a new pod C. The Deployment is deleted too D. The pod is restarted with the same name and IP The ReplicaSet controller reconciles the number of matching pods with the desired count. The replacement is a brand-new pod. kubectl apply answers: unknown field \"spec.template.spec.containers[0].port\" . What is the most likely mistake? A. The cluster is too old B. The image has no ports C. containers must not be a list D. The field is called ports (a list), so the name or its place is wrong Strict decoding reports the exact path of a field the API does not define. Check the spelling and the indentation level against kubectl explain . Why must spec.selector.matchLabels match the labels in spec.template.metadata.labels ? A. The Deployment finds the pods it owns by that selector; a template it could not select would be created endlessly B. It is only a naming convention C. Kubernetes uses the labels as the pod's host name D. Because Services require it Ownership is established through labels. The API server rejects a Deployment whose selector does not match its own template."},{"l":10,"s":"10-pods-and-deployments","t":"Pods, Deployments and Self-Healing","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Deployments Labels and selectors Object management: imperative and declarative kubectl apply"},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Services and DNS Pods come and go, and each new one has a new IP address. A Service gives a changing group of pods one stable name and address. Understanding how a Service finds its pods is what lets you fix it when it finds none. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Expose pods with a Service , understand selectors , endpoints and cluster DNS , and diagnose a Service that does not answer. Why it matters: \"all pods are Running but the app is down\" is the most common Kubernetes support request, and it is almost always a Service problem that kubectl get endpoints reveals in five seconds."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain what a Service is and how it selects pods. Distinguish port , targetPort and nodePort . Choose between ClusterIP, NodePort and LoadBalancer. Use Service DNS names within and across namespaces. Test a Service from inside the cluster and read its endpoints."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The front end must call the API. There are three API pods, their IP addresses change with every rollout, and the front end must neither know nor care. It should simply call http://api:3000 ."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"what-a-service-is","h":"What a Service is","x":"What a Service is A Service is a small object with three jobs: it has a stable virtual IP (the ClusterIP) and DNS name ; it selects pods by label; and it load-balances connections across the selected pods that are ready. apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: api spec: selector: app: api # send traffic to pods with this label ports: - port: 3000 # the port of the Service itself targetPort: 3000 # the port on the pods where traffic is delivered web pod ──► api:3000 ──DNS──► 10.96.0.27:3000 ──► one of the ready pods matching app=api (name) (ClusterIP) 10.244.1.5:3000 10.244.2.4:3000 Endpoints: the Service's actual targets For every Service, Kubernetes maintains a list of the pod IPs that currently match the selector and are ready. That list is the Service's endpoints , and it is the first thing to look at when a Service misbehaves. kubectl get service api kubectl get endpoints api kubectl describe service api # selector, ports and endpoints in one place NAME TYPE CLUSTER-IP EXTERNAL-IP PORT(S) AGE api ClusterIP 10.96.0.27 <none> 3000/TCP 2m NAME ENDPOINTS AGE api 10.244.1.5:3000,10.244.2.4:3000 2m An empty endpoint list is not an error to Kubernetes If the selector matches no pod, or no matching pod is ready, the endpoints are <none> . Nothing warns you. Clients get connection refused from the Service's IP. Selector and labels are compared as exact strings: app: api and app: backend are simply different."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"the-three-port-numbers","h":"The three port numbers","x":"The three port numbers Field Where it applies Example port On the Service. What clients connect to. http://api:3000 targetPort On the pod. Must be a port the container actually listens on. Defaults to port . The app's listening port nodePort On every node (NodePort and LoadBalancer types only), range 30000–32767. http://NODE-IP:30869 A Service can translate: port: 80, targetPort: 8080 lets clients say http://web while the app keeps listening on 8080."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"service-types","h":"Service types","x":"Service types Type Reachable from Use for ClusterIP (default) Inside the cluster only Everything internal: APIs, databases NodePort Also from outside, on a high port of every node Simple external access, test clusters LoadBalancer Also through an external load balancer the cloud provider creates Public entry points in cloud environments For HTTP applications the usual production setup is ClusterIP Services behind one Ingress (Lesson 17)."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"cluster-dns","h":"Cluster DNS","x":"Cluster DNS The cluster runs a DNS server (CoreDNS), and every pod is configured to use it. Each Service gets a name of the form SERVICE.NAMESPACE.svc.cluster.local . From a pod in namespace shop Resolves to api Service api in the same namespace api.payments Service api in namespace payments api.payments.svc.cluster.local The same, fully qualified The short name is namespace-relative http://api:3000 means \"the Service api in my namespace\". A pod in another namespace calling the same URL gets a name-resolution error, or worse, reaches a different Service that happens to have the same name."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"creating-and-testing-a-service","h":"Creating and testing a Service","x":"Creating and testing a Service kubectl expose deployment web --port=80 --target-port=8080 # generates a Service from the Deployment's selector kubectl get service,endpoints web # a ClusterIP is only reachable from inside, so test from a temporary pod kubectl run tmp --rm -it --restart=Never --image=busybox -- wget -qO- http://web # or forward a local port to the Service while you test kubectl port-forward service/web 8080:80 & curl localhost:8080 Why your shell can't curl a ClusterIP ClusterIPs and pod IPs only exist inside the cluster's network. From your workstation they time out. Use a temporary pod, kubectl port-forward , a NodePort, or an Ingress."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"the-service-checklist","h":"The Service checklist","x":"The Service checklist When a Service does not answer, three checks in this order find nearly every cause: Are there endpoints? kubectl get endpoints NAME . None → selector mismatch, or pods not ready (Lesson 14). Do the endpoint ports match what the container listens on? If not, fix targetPort (Lesson 17). Is the client using the right name and port? Same namespace? The Service's port , not the pod's?"},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"scenario-1-give-the-pods-one-stable-address","h":"Scenario 1: give the pods one stable address","x":"Scenario 1: give the pods one stable address This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"scenario-2-every-pod-is-running-and-the-shop-is-still-down","h":"Scenario 2: every pod is Running, and the shop is still down","x":"Scenario 2: every pod is Running, and the shop is still down Nothing is crashing, so there is nothing in the logs. Start with step 1 of the checklist. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A Service is a stable name and IP in front of the ready pods that match its selector. Endpoints are the Service's real targets. Empty endpoints mean no pod matches, or none is ready. port is on the Service, targetPort on the pod, nodePort on the nodes. Short DNS names resolve within the caller's namespace; use name.namespace across namespaces. Test ClusterIP Services from inside the cluster."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz kubectl get endpoints api shows <none> , and the api pods are Running and Ready. What is the most likely cause? A. The Service has the wrong port B. DNS is not working C. The Service's selector does not match the pods' labels D. The pods need a restart Endpoints are built from pods that match the selector and are ready. Ready pods that are not listed are not being selected. A Service has port: 80 and targetPort: 8080 . Which URL does a client pod in the same namespace use? A. http://web (port 80) B. http://web:8080 C. The pod's IP on port 80 D. http://web:30080 Clients talk to the Service's port . The Service forwards to targetPort on the pods. A pod in namespace shop must call the Service api in namespace payments . Which host name works? A. api B. api.payments C. payments.api D. api.shop The pattern is SERVICE.NAMESPACE. The bare name api would look for a Service in the caller's own namespace."},{"l":11,"s":"11-services-and-dns","t":"Services and DNS","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Service DNS for Services and Pods Debug Services"},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"ConfigMaps and Secrets In Docker you passed settings with -e . In Kubernetes, configuration lives in its own objects that pods refer to. A wrong reference produces one of the most confusing pod statuses, so this lesson covers both the feature and its failure. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Store configuration in ConfigMaps and sensitive values in Secrets , consume them from pods, and fix a CreateContainerConfigError . Why it matters: one image must run in every environment, and passwords must not sit in manifests under version control. Both depend on this mechanism, and a typo in a reference stops a pod before it starts, with nothing in the logs."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Create ConfigMaps and Secrets from literals and files. Inject them as environment variables and mount them as files. Explain when a change reaches running pods and when it does not. State accurately what a Secret protects and what it does not. Diagnose CreateContainerConfigError from the pod's events."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation Marketing wants a banner on the shop's front page and wants to change the text without waiting for a new image. Meanwhile the security team has noticed the database password in a Deployment manifest in git and wants it gone."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"configmaps","h":"ConfigMaps","x":"ConfigMaps A ConfigMap is a set of key-value pairs. Values can be short strings or whole files. kubectl create configmap web-config --from-literal=BANNER=\"Free shipping this week\" kubectl create configmap nginx-conf --from-file=default.conf # key = file name, value = file content kubectl get configmap web-config -o yaml apiVersion: v1 kind: ConfigMap metadata: name: web-config data: BANNER: Free shipping this week"},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"using-configuration-in-a-pod","h":"Using configuration in a pod","x":"Using configuration in a pod There are two ways to consume a ConfigMap or Secret, and they behave differently. containers: - name: web image: shop/web:1.0 envFrom: # every key becomes a variable - configMapRef: name: web-config env: - name: DB_PASSWORD # or pick single keys valueFrom: secretKeyRef: name: db-auth key: password containers: - name: web image: nginx:1.29 volumeMounts: - name: conf mountPath: /etc/nginx/conf.d # one file per key appears here volumes: - name: conf configMap: name: nginx-conf Environment variables Mounted files Good for Simple settings, twelve-factor style apps Configuration files, certificates When the ConfigMap changes Running pods keep the old value. Only new pods read the new one. The files are updated in running pods after a short delay; the app must re-read them. If the object or key is missing CreateContainerConfigError Pod stuck in ContainerCreating with a FailedMount event \"I changed the ConfigMap and nothing happened\" Environment variables are read once, when the container starts. Editing a ConfigMap does not restart anything. After changing a value that pods consume as an environment variable, trigger a rollout: kubectl rollout restart deployment/web . A quick way to wire a ConfigMap or Secret into an existing Deployment: kubectl set env deployment/web --from=configmap/web-config # one variable per key kubectl set env deployment/web --list # show what is set"},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"secrets","h":"Secrets","x":"Secrets A Secret works like a ConfigMap, with a separate object type so that access to it can be restricted separately. kubectl create secret generic db-auth --from-literal=password=secret kubectl get secret db-auth -o yaml kubectl get secret db-auth -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d apiVersion: v1 kind: Secret metadata: name: db-auth type: Opaque data: password: c2VjcmV0 # base64 of \"secret\" Base64 is not encryption The value in a Secret is encoded so that it can hold binary data. Anyone can decode it, as the command above shows. What a Secret gives you is a separate object that RBAC can restrict (Lesson 19), that is not printed by kubectl describe , and that the cluster can encrypt at rest if configured to. Committing a Secret manifest to git exposes the password just like plain text would. In a manifest you can write plain text under stringData: and the API server encodes it for you. Under data: you must provide valid base64; a common mistake is echo secret | base64 , which encodes a trailing newline into the password. Use echo -n ."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"when-a-reference-is-wrong","h":"When a reference is wrong","x":"When a reference is wrong Before the kubelet creates a container it resolves every reference in the pod spec. If a ConfigMap, a Secret or a key in one of them does not exist, the container is not created at all: NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE api-7d9c5b8f6d-x2x9z 0/1 CreateContainerConfigError 0 45s Warning Failed 12s (x4 over 45s) kubelet Error: secret \"api-secrets\" not found Event message Meaning secret \"x\" not found / configmap \"x\" not found No object of that name in the pod's namespace . A typo, or it was created in another namespace. couldn't find key K in Secret ns/x The object exists but has no such key. Keys are case-sensitive. MountVolume.SetUp failed … not found The same problem for a volume instead of an environment variable. No logs, by design kubectl logs on such a pod says the container is waiting to start . The container never ran, so it has no output. The evidence is in the pod's events . Remember this split; Lesson 15 turns it into a rule for every pod status."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"scenario-1-configuration-and-secrets-outside-the-image","h":"Scenario 1: configuration and secrets outside the image","x":"Scenario 1: configuration and secrets outside the image This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"scenario-2-createcontainerconfigerror","h":"Scenario 2: CreateContainerConfigError","x":"Scenario 2: CreateContainerConfigError This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways ConfigMaps hold configuration, Secrets hold sensitive values; pods consume both as environment variables or files. Environment variables are fixed at container start: change the ConfigMap, then restart the rollout. Secrets are base64-encoded, not encrypted. Protect them with RBAC and keep them out of git. ConfigMaps and Secrets are namespaced; a pod can only reference those in its own namespace. CreateContainerConfigError = an unresolvable reference. The event names the object or key."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz You edit a ConfigMap that a Deployment consumes through envFrom . The pods still show the old value. Why? A. ConfigMaps are immutable B. Environment variables are read at container start; existing pods are not restarted C. The change needs up to an hour to propagate D. envFrom only works with Secrets Nothing restarts pods when a ConfigMap changes. Run kubectl rollout restart to create new pods that read the new values. What protects the contents of a Kubernetes Secret? A. Strong encryption applied by kubectl create secret B. The base64 encoding C. Nothing at all; Secrets are identical to ConfigMaps D. Access control (RBAC) on the Secret object, plus encryption at rest if the cluster enables it Base64 is an encoding anyone can reverse. The protection comes from who is allowed to read the object. A pod is in CreateContainerConfigError . Where is the explanation? A. In the pod's events ( kubectl describe pod ) B. In kubectl logs C. In kubectl logs --previous D. In the node's status The container was never created, so it produced no logs. The kubelet reports the unresolved reference as an event."},{"l":12,"s":"12-configmaps-and-secrets","t":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading ConfigMaps Secrets Good practices for Kubernetes Secrets Configure a pod to use a ConfigMap"},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Rollouts and Rollbacks Releasing a new version without downtime, and getting back quickly when the new version is bad, is the main reason teams adopt Deployments. This lesson shows what happens during a rollout, how to read a stuck one, and how to undo it. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand the rolling update , follow it with kubectl rollout , and recover from a release that never becomes ready. Why it matters: a failed release at five in the afternoon is the classic moment of pressure. If you know that a stuck rollout is safe, and that one command returns to the previous version, you can act calmly."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Describe how a rolling update replaces pods step by step. Explain maxSurge and maxUnavailable . Follow, inspect and undo a rollout. Explain why a failed rollout usually causes no outage. Diagnose ImagePullBackOff during a release."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation Three replicas of the front end serve customers. Version 1.1 is ready to ship. Tomorrow there will be a 1.2, and that one will have a bug nobody caught in testing."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"what-triggers-a-rollout","h":"What triggers a rollout","x":"What triggers a rollout A rollout starts whenever the pod template of a Deployment changes: the image, an environment variable, a resource limit, a label. Changing only replicas is scaling, not a rollout. kubectl set image deployment/web web=shop/web:1.1 # quick; or edit the file and apply kubectl rollout status deployment/web Waiting for deployment \"web\" rollout to finish: 1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated... Waiting for deployment \"web\" rollout to finish: 2 out of 3 new replicas have been updated... Waiting for deployment \"web\" rollout to finish: 1 old replicas are pending termination... deployment \"web\" successfully rolled out"},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"how-a-rolling-update-works","h":"How a rolling update works","x":"How a rolling update works The Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet for the new template and shifts pods from the old ReplicaSet to the new one in small steps: old RS: ●●● new RS: start old RS: ●●● new RS: ○ one extra pod is started (surge) old RS: ●● new RS: ● the new pod is Ready → one old pod is removed old RS: ●● new RS: ●○ next new pod starts ... old RS: new RS: ●●● done; the old ReplicaSet stays, scaled to 0 Setting Meaning Default maxSurge How many pods above the desired count may exist during the update. 25% (rounded up) maxUnavailable How many pods below the desired count may be unavailable. 25% (rounded down) With three replicas that means: at most one extra pod, and never fewer than three available. The decisive rule is that an old pod is only removed after a new pod has become Ready . A stuck rollout is a safe rollout If the new version cannot start (bad image tag, crash at startup, failing readiness probe), its first pod never becomes Ready, so no old pod is removed. The release hangs, the old version keeps serving, and customers notice nothing. This protection depends on a meaningful readiness probe (Lesson 14): without one, a pod counts as ready the moment its process starts."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"history-and-rollback","h":"History and rollback","x":"History and rollback Old ReplicaSets are kept (ten by default) precisely so that you can go back. kubectl rollout history deployment/web kubectl rollout history deployment/web --revision=2 # what that revision's pod template was kubectl rollout undo deployment/web # back to the previous revision kubectl rollout undo deployment/web --to-revision=1 deployment.apps/web REVISION CHANGE-CAUSE 1 <none> 2 <none> undo does not build or pull anything new: it sets the Deployment's pod template back to that of an earlier ReplicaSet, and a normal rolling update towards the old version follows. The old revision is re-issued under a new number, so after undoing revision 3 the history lists 1, 3 and 4: what used to be revision 2 is now revision 4. ❌ Under pressure Deleting the failing pods, editing things live, or scaling the Deployment down and up. The broken template is still there, so the same pods come straight back. ✅ What works kubectl rollout undo , confirm with rollout status , and then find the cause from the failed pod's events and logs without time pressure."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"reading-a-stuck-rollout","h":"Reading a stuck rollout","x":"Reading a stuck rollout NAME READY UP-TO-DATE AVAILABLE AGE web 3/3 1 3 12m NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE web-c6g9q7brjz-dwx29 1/1 Running 0 12m web-c6g9q7brjz-mvjrq 1/1 Running 0 12m web-c6g9q7brjz-vvxfp 1/1 Running 0 12m web-rhrcvrmcl2-pb8kr 0/1 ImagePullBackOff 0 90s Column Meaning here READY 3/3 Three pods are ready (the old ones). Customers are fine. UP-TO-DATE 1 Only one pod has the new template. Two different hashes in the pod names Two ReplicaSets are active: a rollout is in progress. The new pod's status tells you what kind of failure it is. ImagePullBackOff is the most frequent one during releases: Normal Pulling 8s (x2 over 20s) kubelet Pulling image \"nginx:9.99\" Warning Failed 6s (x2 over 18s) kubelet Failed to pull image \"nginx:9.99\": … docker.io/library/nginx:9.99: not found Warning Failed 6s (x2 over 18s) kubelet Error: ErrImagePull Normal BackOff 4s (x2 over 16s) kubelet Back-off pulling image \"nginx:9.99\" Registry's answer in the event Cause …: not found / manifest unknown The repository exists but the tag does not: a typo, or the image was never pushed. pull access denied, repository does not exist or may require authorization A typo in the image name , or a private repository without credentials ( imagePullSecrets ). 401 Unauthorized Private registry; the pull secret is missing or wrong. ErrImagePull and ImagePullBackOff are one problem ErrImagePull is the failed attempt; ImagePullBackOff is the kubelet waiting before the next attempt, with pauses that grow up to five minutes. The pod alternates between the two. Fixing the image reference fixes both."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"scenario-1-roll-forward-hit-a-bad-release-roll-back","h":"Scenario 1: roll forward, hit a bad release, roll back","x":"Scenario 1: roll forward, hit a bad release, roll back This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"scenario-2-the-pipeline-says-timed-out-waiting-for-the-condition","h":"Scenario 2: the pipeline says \"timed out waiting for the condition\"","x":"Scenario 2: the pipeline says \"timed out waiting for the condition\" First establish whether customers are affected. Then find the cause. Then clean up. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A change to the pod template starts a rollout: a new ReplicaSet grows while the old one shrinks. Old pods are removed only when new ones are Ready, so a release that cannot start stalls safely. kubectl rollout status follows, history lists, undo returns to an earlier template. READY high and UP-TO-DATE low means: stuck rollout, old version still serving. ImagePullBackOff : read the registry's answer in the pod's events; it distinguishes a wrong tag from a wrong name or missing credentials."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A Deployment with 4 replicas is updated to an image tag that does not exist. With default settings, what do customers experience? A. Nothing: the old pods keep serving because no new pod becomes Ready B. A full outage until someone rolls back C. Half of the requests fail D. The Deployment is deleted The rolling update only removes old pods as new ones become ready. A pod in ImagePullBackOff never does. Which change triggers a rollout? A. Changing spec.replicas from 3 to 5 B. Adding a label to the Deployment's own metadata C. Changing an environment variable in the pod template D. Running kubectl get deployment Only changes to spec.template create a new ReplicaSet. Changing replicas just scales the current one. What does kubectl rollout undo deployment/web do? A. Deletes the newest pods B. Sets the pod template back to the previous revision's, which starts a rolling update towards it C. Restores a backup of the cluster D. Restarts the pods with the same template The previous ReplicaSet still exists with its template. Undo points the Deployment back at it."},{"l":13,"s":"13-rollouts-and-rollbacks","t":"Rollouts and Rollbacks","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Updating a Deployment Rolling back a Deployment Images: pull policy, private registries and ImagePullBackOff"},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits Kubernetes can only keep an application healthy if it knows what \"healthy\" means and how much the application needs. You tell it with probes and with resource settings. Both are powerful, and both are a frequent source of self-inflicted outages. ⏱ 45 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Configure readiness and liveness probes and resource requests and limits , and diagnose OOMKilled containers and pods that are Running but not Ready . Why it matters: without probes, broken pods receive traffic. With wrong probes, healthy pods are cut off or killed. Without requests the scheduler is blind; with limits that are too low the kernel kills your process. Getting these four settings right prevents a large share of production incidents."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain what readiness and liveness probes do when they fail. Write HTTP, TCP and exec probes. Explain the difference between requests and limits, for CPU and for memory. Recognise and fix an OOMKilled container. Explain \"Running, 0/1 Ready\" and find the cause."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation Two changes were made to the shop this week \"to make it more robust\": probes on the front end, and memory limits on the API. One of each was configured wrongly, and both took the shop down."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"probes","h":"Probes","x":"Probes A probe is a check the kubelet runs against a container at regular intervals. Probe Question When it fails Readiness Can this pod take requests right now? The pod is removed from every Service's endpoints. It keeps running and is not restarted. Liveness Is this container stuck beyond recovery? The container is killed and restarted . Startup Has a slow-starting app finished starting? Liveness and readiness are held back until it succeeds. containers: - name: web image: shop/web:1.0 ports: - containerPort: 8080 readinessProbe: httpGet: path: /healthz port: 8080 periodSeconds: 5 livenessProbe: httpGet: path: /healthz port: 8080 initialDelaySeconds: 10 failureThreshold: 3 Mechanism Succeeds when httpGet The response status is between 200 and 399. tcpSocket The port accepts a connection. exec The command exits with code 0. Running is not Ready The READY column of kubectl get pods counts containers that pass their readiness probe. A pod can be Running and 0/1 at the same time: NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE web-jm5q9ndbwk-7kxvh 0/1 Running 0 3m Warning Unhealthy 5s (x36 over 3m) kubelet Readiness probe failed: Get \"http://10.244.1.7:80/healthz\": dial tcp 10.244.1.7:80: connect: connection refused The message contains everything: which probe, the exact URL, and the error class you know from Lesson 6. Connection refused on port 80 for an app that listens on 8080 is a wrong port in the probe, not a broken app. statuscode: 404 would be a wrong path; statuscode: 503 would be the application itself declaring that it is not ready. ❌ Probes that cause outages A liveness probe that checks a dependency (the database). When the database hiccups, every pod is restarted at once. A liveness probe with too short a delay on a slow-starting app: killed before it finishes starting, forever. A probe pointing at the wrong port or path. ✅ Probes that help Readiness: \"can I serve a request?\", including the dependencies you can't work without. Liveness: only \"is this process hopelessly stuck?\". Keep it cheap and independent of other services. Slow start: a startupProbe instead of a huge initialDelaySeconds ."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"requests-and-limits","h":"Requests and limits","x":"Requests and limits Each container can state what it needs and what it may use at most. resources: requests: # used by the SCHEDULER to place the pod cpu: 100m # 100 millicores = a tenth of a CPU memory: 128Mi limits: # ENFORCED on the node while the container runs memory: 256Mi Request Limit Used by The scheduler: a node must have this much unreserved The kubelet and the kernel, at run time CPU exceeded A request is a reservation, not a cap The container is throttled (slowed down), never killed Memory exceeded The container is killed : OOMKilled , exit code 137 If omitted The scheduler assumes zero: pods may be packed onto a node that cannot hold them No ceiling: one leaking pod can starve its neighbours Units: CPU in cores ( 0.5 ) or millicores ( 500m ); memory in bytes with suffixes, where Mi and Gi are powers of two. Beware of 128m for memory: that is 0.128 bytes , a typo for 128Mi . The combination of requests and limits determines the pod's QoS class , which decides who is evicted first when a node runs out of memory: Guaranteed (requests equal limits for everything), Burstable (some set), BestEffort (none set; first to go). OOMKilled State: Waiting Reason: CrashLoopBackOff Last State: Terminated Reason: OOMKilled Exit Code: 137 Restart Count: 6 Limits: memory: 64Mi You have met exit code 137 in Lesson 8. In Kubernetes the reason is spelled out: the container exceeded its memory limit and the kernel killed it. The application's log shows nothing unusual, because a killed process does not get to write a last line. kubectl top pods # current usage (needs the metrics server) kubectl set resources deployment/api --requests=memory=128Mi --limits=memory=256Mi Size limits from measurements Measure the working set under realistic load with kubectl top pods , add headroom, and set the limit there. Raising the limit cures an undersized limit. It only postpones the kill if the application leaks memory."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"scenario-1-add-probes-and-resources","h":"Scenario 1: add probes and resources","x":"Scenario 1: add probes and resources This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"scenario-2-oomkilled","h":"Scenario 2: OOMKilled","x":"Scenario 2: OOMKilled This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"scenario-3-running-but-0-1-ready","h":"Scenario 3: Running, but 0/1 Ready","x":"Scenario 3: Running, but 0/1 Ready This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Readiness failing: no traffic, no restart. Liveness failing: restart. \"Running 0/1\" means the readiness probe fails; the Unhealthy event gives the URL and the error. Requests are for scheduling; limits are enforced. CPU over the limit is throttled, memory over the limit is killed. OOMKilled / exit code 137: compare the memory limit with real usage ( kubectl top pods ). A wrong probe can take down a healthy application. Keep liveness probes simple."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A pod's readiness probe has been failing for ten minutes. What has Kubernetes done? A. Restarted the container several times B. Removed the pod from Service endpoints; the container keeps running C. Deleted the pod and created a new one D. Marked the node as NotReady Readiness controls traffic only. Restarts are the liveness probe's job. A container has limits.memory: 64Mi and its process needs 120 MiB. What happens? A. It is slowed down B. It is moved to a bigger node C. The limit is raised automatically D. It is killed (OOMKilled, exit code 137) and restarted, over and over Memory can't be throttled. Exceeding the limit triggers the kernel's OOM killer. Why is it risky to let a liveness probe check the database connection? A. A short database outage would restart every pod at once, turning a hiccup into a full outage B. Liveness probes can't open network connections C. The database would be restarted D. It disables the readiness probe Liveness should only detect a process that cannot recover by itself. Dependency checks belong in readiness."},{"l":14,"s":"14-probes-and-resources","t":"Health Probes, Requests and Limits","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Liveness, readiness and startup probes Resource management for pods and containers Pod Quality of Service classes"},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs You have fixed several broken pods by now, each with a hint about where to look. This lesson removes the hints. It gives you one loop and one table that together tell you, for any pod status, where the explanation is. ⏱ 45 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Diagnose any unhealthy pod with the loop get → describe → logs , using the pod's status to decide where the evidence is. Why it matters: there are only about a dozen ways a pod fails to run. Each shows a characteristic status, and each status has one place where the cause is written down. Learn the mapping once and you can debug clusters you have never seen."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Run the debugging loop without prompting. Say for each common pod status whether the cause is in the events or in the logs. Use kubectl logs --previous , -c and label selectors. Explain what \"BackOff\" means and why it is never the root cause. Debug a pod whose command cannot be started."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"the-loop","h":"The loop","x":"The loop ┌─► 1. kubectl get pods What is the STATUS? How many RESTARTS? How old? │ 2. kubectl describe pod NAME State / Last State / Exit Code, and the Events at the bottom │ 3. kubectl logs NAME What did the application say? (--previous for the last crash) │ 4. Form ONE hypothesis, change ONE thing └── 5. Watch the status change, and go round again It is the method from Lesson 8 with Kubernetes commands. Step 1 tells you whether step 2 or step 3 has the answer."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"a-pod-s-life-and-where-it-can-stop","h":"A pod's life, and where it can stop","x":"A pod's life, and where it can stop created ─► scheduled ─► image pulled ─► config resolved ─► container started ─► running ─► ready │ │ │ │ │ │ │ rejected Pending ErrImagePull / CreateContainer- RunContainerError CrashLoop- Running by API ImagePullBackOff ConfigError BackOff, but 0/1 OOMKilled └──────────── no container yet: read EVENTS ───────────┘└── container ran: read LOGS ──┘ That last line is the rule. If no container has run, there are no logs: the kubelet or scheduler reports the reason as an event. If a container ran and died, the application's own output has the reason."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"the-status-table","h":"The status table","x":"The status table STATUS Meaning Evidence in Usual causes Pending No node assigned yet. Events: FailedScheduling Requests too large, node selector, taints, unbound volume (Lesson 16) ContainerCreating (for long) Node assigned; waiting for volumes or network. Events: FailedMount Missing ConfigMap/Secret volume, unbound claim ErrImagePull / ImagePullBackOff The image can't be pulled. Events: Failed to pull image … Wrong name or tag, private registry (Lesson 13) InvalidImageName The image reference isn't even syntactically valid. Events Upper-case letters, a trailing colon, stray spaces CreateContainerConfigError A referenced ConfigMap, Secret or key doesn't exist. Events: Error: … not found Typo in name or key, wrong namespace (Lesson 12) RunContainerError The runtime couldn't start the process. Events: executable file not found Wrong command / args , binary missing from the image CrashLoopBackOff The container starts, exits, and is restarted with growing pauses. Logs (and --previous ); Last State in describe Application error, missing env var, unreachable dependency at startup OOMKilled Killed for exceeding its memory limit. describe: Last State, Limits Limit too low, memory leak (Lesson 14) Error / Completed The container exited with a non-zero / zero code and isn't being restarted (yet). Logs Jobs; or a server whose command ends immediately Running , READY 0/1 Alive but failing its readiness probe. Events: Unhealthy Wrong probe, app not ready, dependency down (Lesson 14) Init:… An init container hasn't finished. Same statuses, prefixed. kubectl logs NAME -c INIT-CONTAINER The init container's own failure Terminating (for long) Deletion requested; the container is slow to stop. describe Process ignores SIGTERM; finalizers \"BackOff\" is the waiting, not the problem CrashLoopBackOff and ImagePullBackOff both mean: \"this keeps failing, so I wait longer before each retry\" (10 s, 20 s, 40 s … up to 5 minutes). Searching the web for \"CrashLoopBackOff\" leads nowhere, because it describes Kubernetes' reaction. The cause is one step earlier: the crash, or the failed pull."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"getting-the-right-logs","h":"Getting the right logs","x":"Getting the right logs kubectl logs web-5d4f8c7b9-x2x9z # current container instance kubectl logs web-5d4f8c7b9-x2x9z --previous # the instance before the last restart kubectl logs web-5d4f8c7b9-x2x9z -c sidecar # a specific container in a multi-container pod kubectl logs deployment/web # one pod of the Deployment (kubectl picks it) kubectl logs -l app=web --tail=20 # the last lines of every pod with that label kubectl logs -f deployment/web # follow kubectl says Meaning container \"x\" in pod \"y\" is waiting to start: ContainerCreating (or trying and failing to pull image ) No container has run yet. Go to the events. previous terminated container \"x\" … not found The container has not been restarted, so there is no previous instance. Empty output The process wrote nothing to stdout or stderr. It may log to a file inside the container, or it was killed before it could log."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"describe-read-properly","h":"describe, read properly","x":"describe, read properly Three places in kubectl describe pod carry most of the information: Containers: api: Image: shop/api:1.0 ← is this the image you expected? State: Waiting Reason: CrashLoopBackOff Last State: Terminated ← how the previous attempt ended Reason: Error Exit Code: 1 Restart Count: 5 Environment: <none> ← what the container was given ... Events: ← what the scheduler and kubelet did, and why they stopped Warning BackOff 3s (x12 over 2m) kubelet Back-off restarting failed container api in pod … Events are kept for about an hour and are aggregated: (x12 over 2m) means the same event occurred twelve times within two minutes. For the whole namespace in time order, use kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp or kubectl events . When the pod won't stay up long enough to look inside You can't kubectl exec into a container that keeps crashing. Reproduce the environment instead: start a temporary pod from the same image with a harmless command ( kubectl run debug --rm -it --restart=Never --image=IMAGE --command -- sh ) and inspect files and environment there, or compare the pod's -o yaml with a working one."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"scenario-three-pods-three-different-failures","h":"Scenario: three pods, three different failures","x":"Scenario: three pods, three different failures No per-pod story this time. Use the status table to decide, for each Deployment, whether to read events or logs, and fix the Deployment (not the pod). This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways get → describe → logs → one change → repeat. No container has run (Pending, ImagePull…, CreateContainerConfigError, RunContainerError): the cause is in the events . A container ran and exited (CrashLoopBackOff, Error, OOMKilled): the cause is in the logs and in Last State . \"BackOff\" is Kubernetes waiting between retries. Look for the failure that precedes it. Fix the owner (Deployment), never the individual pod."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A pod is in ImagePullBackOff . You run kubectl logs and get \"container is waiting to start\". What now? A. Wait for the logs to appear B. Add --previous C. Read the events with kubectl describe pod : no container has run, so there are no logs D. Restart the node The image was never pulled, so no container ever started. The registry's answer is recorded as an event. A pod shows CrashLoopBackOff with 8 restarts, and kubectl logs prints only a start-up line. Which command most likely shows the error? A. kubectl logs POD --previous B. kubectl get pod POD -o wide C. kubectl top pod POD D. kubectl get nodes The current instance has only just started. The crash message belongs to the previous instance. What is the correct way to fix a pod that fails because its Deployment's template has a wrong environment variable? A. Edit the pod B. Delete the pod repeatedly until it works C. kubectl exec into it and export the variable D. Change the Deployment; it will replace the pod with a corrected one Pods of a Deployment are created from its template. Any direct change to a pod is lost with the next replacement."},{"l":15,"s":"15-the-debugging-loop","t":"The Debugging Loop: Get, Describe, Logs","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Debug pods Debug running pods Pod lifecycle kubectl logs"},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes A Pending pod is a pod the scheduler could not place. There is no container, no log and no crash, only one event with a sentence that looks cryptic and is in fact a complete explanation. This lesson teaches you to read that sentence. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand how the scheduler chooses a node, decode FailedScheduling messages, and manage nodes with cordon , drain , labels and taints . Why it matters: Pending pods appear exactly when you scale up for load or lose a node, the moments when you most need capacity. The fix differs completely depending on the reason, and the reason is written in the event."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Describe the scheduler's filter-then-score process. Read a 0/3 nodes are available: … message reason by reason. Explain why an idle cluster can still report Insufficient cpu . Use node selectors, taints and tolerations. Take a node out of service and return it safely."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation Two workloads have been Pending for ten minutes, a node is still marked for a maintenance that ended yesterday, and somebody needs to patch worker-1 tonight without an outage."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"how-the-scheduler-decides","h":"How the scheduler decides","x":"How the scheduler decides For every pod without a node, the scheduler works in two phases: Filter : discard every node that cannot run the pod. Not enough unreserved CPU or memory, a node selector that doesn't match, a taint the pod doesn't tolerate, the node is cordoned, a required volume isn't available there. Score : rank the remaining nodes (spread pods out, prefer nodes that already have the image, and so on) and pick the best. If the filter phase leaves no node, the pod stays Pending and the scheduler records why, counting nodes per reason: Warning FailedScheduling 2m default-scheduler 0/3 nodes are available: 1 Insufficient cpu, 1 node(s) had untolerated taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: }, 1 node(s) were unschedulable. preemption: 0/3 nodes are available: 1 No preemption victims found for incoming pod, 2 Preemption is not helpful for scheduling. Read it as a list. The numbers add up to the number of nodes, and each node is excluded for one reason: Fragment Meaning Fix Insufficient cpu / Insufficient memory The pod's requests don't fit into what is still unreserved on that node. Lower the request, free capacity, or add nodes node(s) didn't match Pod's node affinity/selector The pod demands a node label that node lacks. Label a node, or correct the selector node(s) had untolerated taint {key: value} The node repels pods that don't tolerate this taint. Add a toleration, or remove the taint node(s) were unschedulable The node is cordoned. kubectl uncordon pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims The pod's volume claim isn't bound. Fix the claim (Lesson 18) Too many pods The node has reached its pod limit. Add nodes The part after preemption: says whether evicting lower-priority pods would have helped. You can usually ignore it. The control-plane taint is normal Almost every message includes untolerated taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: } . Control-plane nodes are reserved for the cluster's own components. That fragment is background noise; the other reasons are the ones to act on."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"requests-not-usage","h":"Requests, not usage","x":"Requests, not usage The scheduler does not look at how busy a node is . It adds up the requests of the pods already assigned to the node and compares that sum with the node's allocatable capacity. kubectl describe node worker-1 Allocatable: cpu: 2 memory: 4Gi pods: 110 ... Allocated resources: Resource Requests Limits -------- -------- ------ cpu 450m (22%) 0 (0%) memory 448Mi (10%) 896Mi (21%) ❌ The puzzling case \" kubectl top nodes shows 5% CPU usage, and my pod is Pending with Insufficient cpu .\" ✅ The explanation Usage is irrelevant to scheduling. Either the existing pods have reserved the capacity through their requests, or this pod requests more than any single node has. A request of cpu: 3 can never be placed on 2-CPU nodes."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"steering-pods-selectors-taints-tolerations","h":"Steering pods: selectors, taints, tolerations","x":"Steering pods: selectors, taints, tolerations Mechanism Set on Effect nodeSelector Pod \"Only run me on nodes with these labels.\" Pod is attracted to nodes. Taint Node \"Keep away unless you tolerate me.\" Node repels pods. Toleration Pod \"I can live with that taint.\" Permission, not attraction. kubectl get nodes --show-labels kubectl label node worker-1 disktype=ssd # add a label kubectl label node worker-1 disktype- # remove it kubectl taint nodes worker-2 dedicated=batch:NoSchedule # add a taint kubectl taint nodes worker-2 dedicated:NoSchedule- # remove it spec: nodeSelector: disktype: ssd tolerations: - key: dedicated operator: Equal value: batch effect: NoSchedule"},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"node-maintenance-cordon-and-drain","h":"Node maintenance: cordon and drain","x":"Node maintenance: cordon and drain kubectl cordon worker-1 # no new pods; existing ones keep running kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets # cordon + evict the pods # ... do the maintenance ... kubectl uncordon worker-1 # schedulable again NAME STATUS ROLES AGE VERSION cp-1 Ready control-plane 4d v1.37.0 worker-1 Ready,SchedulingDisabled <none> 4d v1.37.0 worker-2 Ready <none> 4d v1.37.0 drain refuses because of… Why Flag DaemonSet-managed pods Their controller would recreate them on the same node at once. --ignore-daemonsets Pods with local storage ( emptyDir ) Their data is deleted with the pod. --delete-emptydir-data Pods without a controller Nobody will recreate them. --force (they are gone for good) Two things people forget Uncordon. A node left in SchedulingDisabled silently removes a third of your capacity until the day you scale up and pods go Pending. Nothing moves back. After uncordon the node is empty and stays empty; the scheduler only places new pods. The next rollout redistributes them."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"scenario-1-take-a-node-out-of-service-without-an-outage","h":"Scenario 1: take a node out of service without an outage","x":"Scenario 1: take a node out of service without an outage This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"scenario-2-pending-forever","h":"Scenario 2: Pending forever","x":"Scenario 2: Pending forever Two Pending workloads with different reasons, and a leftover from last week. Decode each event fragment by fragment. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Pending = the scheduler found no node. The FailedScheduling event lists one reason per excluded node. The scheduler compares requests with unreserved capacity, never actual usage. Node selectors attract, taints repel, tolerations permit. cordon stops new pods, drain also evicts, uncordon reverses it. Pods don't move back by themselves. The control-plane taint in the message is expected; act on the other reasons."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz kubectl top nodes shows all nodes nearly idle, yet a pod is Pending with Insufficient cpu . What explains this? A. The metrics are wrong B. Scheduling is based on the sum of CPU requests, not on current usage C. The pod has no CPU limit D. The scheduler is down Capacity is reserved by requests. Idle but fully reserved nodes can't accept more, and a single request larger than a node can never fit. A node has the taint dedicated=batch:NoSchedule . Which pods can be scheduled there? A. Only pods with a matching toleration B. Only pods with nodeSelector: {dedicated: batch} C. All pods, but with lower priority D. No pods at all A taint repels everything that doesn't tolerate it. A node selector is a separate mechanism; to force pods onto the node you would use both. After kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets and the maintenance, what must you do? A. Nothing; the node returns to service by itself B. Delete and recreate the node C. Restart all Deployments immediately D. kubectl uncordon worker-1 , otherwise the node stays unschedulable Drain leaves the node cordoned. Until you uncordon it, the scheduler ignores it."},{"l":16,"s":"16-pending-pods","t":"Pending Pods: Scheduling, Taints and Nodes","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Kubernetes scheduler Assigning pods to nodes Taints and tolerations Safely drain a node"},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies A request crosses several hops between a user and a container, and any of them can be misconfigured while every pod reports Running. This lesson gives you a checklist that walks the path hop by hop, and adds the two objects at its edges: Ingress and NetworkPolicy. ⏱ 50 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Debug connectivity systematically from Ingress through Service to pod , and recognise the signature of a NetworkPolicy . Why it matters: networking faults produce no crashing pod and no log line. The only evidence is the error the client gets, and a handful of objects you must know to inspect. With a fixed order of checks, these become some of the quickest problems to solve."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Walk a request's path and test each hop. Diagnose selector, readiness and targetPort problems from endpoints. Create an Ingress and explain what 404 and 503 from the ingress controller mean. Explain how a NetworkPolicy changes a pod's behaviour. Tell \"refused\" from \"timed out\" and say which layer each points to."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"the-path-of-a-request","h":"The path of a request","x":"The path of a request user ──► DNS ──► ingress controller ──► Service ──► endpoints ──► pod:port ──► container (1) (2) (3) (4) (5) host/path rule selector ready pods targetPort = → backend Service → pods only listening port? Debug from the inside out: prove the pod works, then the Service, then the Ingress. Each step that passes shrinks the search."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"the-checklist","h":"The checklist","x":"The checklist # Check Command If it fails 1 Are the pods Running and Ready ? kubectl get pods -o wide Lessons 14 and 15 2 Does the app answer on its own port? kubectl exec POD -- wget -qO- localhost:8080 or kubectl port-forward pod/POD 8080:8080 The app, or the port it listens on 3 Does the Service have endpoints? kubectl get endpoints SVC Selector does not match, or pods not ready 4 Do the endpoint ports equal the listening port? Compare with step 2 Wrong targetPort 5 Does the Service answer from another pod? kubectl run tmp --rm -it --restart=Never --image=busybox -- wget -qO- http://SVC Name/namespace, Service port , or a NetworkPolicy 6 Does the Ingress route to it? kubectl describe ingress NAME , curl -i http://HOST/ Class, host, path, backend The error class, once more Client sees Layer to suspect Can't resolve the name Service name or namespace wrong; Service doesn't exist Connection refused, from the Service IP No endpoints (step 3), or endpoints on a port nobody listens on (step 4) Timeout A NetworkPolicy dropping packets; a Service port that doesn't exist HTTP 404 / 503 from the proxy Ingress: no matching rule / no healthy backend HTTP 5xx from the app The application or its dependencies; read its logs"},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"ingress","h":"Ingress","x":"Ingress A Service of type ClusterIP is only reachable inside the cluster. An Ingress publishes HTTP services to the outside under host names and paths, through one shared entry point. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: shop spec: ingressClassName: traefik # which controller should handle this rules: - host: shop.local http: paths: - path: / pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: web # a Service in the SAME namespace port: number: 80 # the Service's port, not the pod's An Ingress is only a set of rules. The work is done by an ingress controller : a reverse proxy running in the cluster that watches Ingress objects and configures itself. Without a controller that claims the Ingress's class, the object exists and nothing happens. kubectl get ingressclass kubectl get ingress # ADDRESS empty = no controller picked it up kubectl describe ingress shop # each rule, resolved to backend endpoints curl -i http://shop.local/ Rules: Host Path Backends ---- ---- -------- shop.local / web:80 (10.244.1.3:8080,10.244.2.3:8080) Symptom Meaning Look at ADDRESS column stays empty No controller handles this Ingress. ingressClassName versus kubectl get ingressclass 404 from the proxy The controller has no rule for this host and path. Host header, path, class 503 from the proxy A rule matched, but its backend has no ready endpoints or doesn't exist. describe ingress : backend Service name and port; then the Service checklist Which controller, and what about the Gateway API? The sandbox cluster runs Traefik with the class traefik , so its error pages say 404 page not found and no available server . Other controllers word them differently, but the meaning of 404 and 503 is the same. The widely used community controller ingress-nginx was retired in March 2026 ; clusters that relied on it are moving to other controllers or to the Gateway API ( Gateway and HTTPRoute objects), which is the successor to Ingress for new setups. The Ingress API itself remains stable and supported, and the debugging steps here carry over directly."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"networkpolicy","h":"NetworkPolicy","x":"NetworkPolicy By default every pod can talk to every other pod in the cluster. A NetworkPolicy restricts that. The rule that surprises people: Selected means isolated As soon as any NetworkPolicy selects a pod for ingress, that pod accepts only the connections that some policy explicitly allows. Everything else is dropped silently. Policies are additive allow-lists; there is no \"deny\" rule. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny-ingress spec: podSelector: {} # every pod in this namespace policyTypes: [Ingress] --- apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: allow-api-from-web spec: podSelector: matchLabels: app: api # protects the api pods policyTypes: [Ingress] ingress: - from: - podSelector: matchLabels: app: web # only pods with this label may connect ports: - port: 3000 kubectl get networkpolicy kubectl describe networkpolicy allow-api-from-web kubectl get pods --show-labels # do the labels in \"from\" match real pods? Because dropped packets get no reply, the client waits and then reports a timeout . Between two healthy pods with populated endpoints, a timeout is the fingerprint of a NetworkPolicy. And like Services, policies are tied to pods by labels only: a from selector with a label no pod carries is perfectly valid and allows nobody."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"scenario-1-route-outside-traffic-with-an-ingress","h":"Scenario 1: route outside traffic with an Ingress","x":"Scenario 1: route outside traffic with an Ingress This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"scenario-2-endpoints-exist-connection-refused","h":"Scenario 2: endpoints exist, connection refused","x":"Scenario 2: endpoints exist, connection refused Step 3 of the checklist passes. Go on to step 4. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"scenario-3-404-page-not-found","h":"Scenario 3: 404 page not found","x":"Scenario 3: 404 page not found This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"scenario-4-it-worked-until-we-secured-the-namespace","h":"Scenario 4: it worked until we \"secured\" the namespace","x":"Scenario 4: it worked until we \"secured\" the namespace The hardest of the four. Note the error class before you look at anything else. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Check in order: pod ready → app answers locally → endpoints exist → ports match → Service from another pod → Ingress. Refused at a Service: no endpoints or wrong targetPort . Timeout between healthy pods: NetworkPolicy. An Ingress needs a controller that claims its class. Empty ADDRESS = nobody did. 404 from the proxy = no rule matched; 503 = rule matched, backend unavailable. A pod selected by any NetworkPolicy accepts only what policies allow. Policies match by label."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz kubectl get endpoints web shows 10.244.1.3:80,10.244.2.3:80 , the pods are Ready, and requests to the Service are refused. What is the most likely cause? A. The selector matches no pods B. DNS is broken C. targetPort is 80, but the containers listen on another port D. A NetworkPolicy Endpoints exist, so selection and readiness are fine. The endpoint port comes from targetPort ; if the app listens elsewhere, the connection is refused. An Ingress has had no ADDRESS for an hour. What do you check first? A. The pods' logs B. Whether its ingressClassName matches a class that a running controller handles C. The Service's selector D. The node's disk space A controller only acts on Ingresses of its class. An unclaimed Ingress gets no address and no route. After adding NetworkPolicies, calls from web to api hang and then time out. Pods and endpoints are healthy. Why a timeout? A. A policy silently drops the packets, so the client gets no answer at all B. The api pods are too slow C. The Service has no endpoints D. The image is wrong \"Refused\" needs something to send a refusal. A policy drop sends nothing, so the client waits until its timeout."},{"l":17,"s":"17-broken-networking","t":"Broken Networking: Services, Ingress, Policies","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Debug Services Ingress Gateway API Network Policies"},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Storage and Stateful Apps A pod's file system dies with the pod, just as a container's did in Lesson 5. Kubernetes solves this with three objects that are easy to mix up. This lesson sorts them out and follows the chain that leads from a Pending database pod to a misspelt storage class. ⏱ 45 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand PersistentVolumeClaims , PersistentVolumes and StorageClasses , run a database with persistent storage, and know when to use a StatefulSet . Why it matters: storage problems stop pods before they start, and the error sits two objects away from the pod. Data is also the one thing in a cluster you cannot recreate from a manifest, so the deletion rules matter."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain how claim, volume and storage class relate. Give a pod persistent storage through a claim. Explain why a claim can be Pending, including WaitForFirstConsumer . Describe what a StatefulSet adds over a Deployment. Trace a Pending pod to its unbound claim and fix it."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The database moves into the cluster. Its pod will be rescheduled during node maintenance and replaced on upgrades, and the orders must be there every time it comes back."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"three-objects","h":"Three objects","x":"Three objects Pod ──mounts──► PersistentVolumeClaim ──bound to──► PersistentVolume ──► real storage \"I need 1Gi, read-write\" \"here is a 1Gi disk\" (cloud disk, NFS, (yours, namespaced) (cluster-wide) local directory …) ▲ ▲ └─── StorageClass: creates a matching volume on demand ───┘ Object Who creates it What it is PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) You, next to your app A request for storage: size, access mode, optionally a class. PersistentVolume (PV) Usually the cluster, automatically An actual piece of storage that a claim can be bound to. StorageClass The cluster administrator A recipe for creating volumes on demand (\"dynamic provisioning\"). apiVersion: v1 kind: PersistentVolumeClaim metadata: name: db-data spec: accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce] # mountable read-write by one node at a time resources: requests: storage: 1Gi # storageClassName: standard # omit to use the cluster's default class spec: containers: - name: db image: postgres:17 volumeMounts: - name: data mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data volumes: - name: data persistentVolumeClaim: claimName: db-data kubectl get storageclass kubectl get pvc kubectl get pv kubectl describe pvc db-data # events explain a Pending claim NAME PROVISIONER RECLAIMPOLICY VOLUMEBINDINGMODE ALLOWVOLUMEEXPANSION AGE standard (default) rancher.io/local-path Delete WaitForFirstConsumer false 4d NAME STATUS VOLUME CAPACITY ACCESS MODES STORAGECLASS VOLUMEATTRIBUTESCLASS AGE db-data Bound pvc-8fa99877-4d56-6478-22ef-e98627da06f1 1Gi RWO standard <unset> 2m"},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"why-a-claim-is-pending","h":"Why a claim is Pending","x":"Why a claim is Pending Event on the claim Meaning Action waiting for first consumer to be created before binding Normal. The class uses WaitForFirstConsumer : the volume is created once a pod that uses the claim is scheduled, so that it lands on the right node. None. Create the pod. storageclass.storage.k8s.io \"X\" not found The claim names a class this cluster doesn't have. Use an existing class (see below: this needs a new claim). no persistent volumes available for this claim and no storage class is set No default class, and no pre-created volume fits. Set a class, or create a matching volume. And the pod waits for the claim. That gives the chain you follow when a pod with storage is Pending: kubectl describe pod db-0 → \"pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims\" kubectl get pvc → data-db-0 Pending … fast-ssd kubectl describe pvc data-db-0 → storageclass.storage.k8s.io \"fast-ssd\" not found kubectl get storageclass → only \"standard\" exists A claim's class and size request are (mostly) fixed You can't change the storage class of an existing claim; the API server answers Forbidden: spec is immutable after creation . To correct a wrong class on a claim that never bound, delete the claim and create it again. For a claim that holds data, that means a migration, so get it right the first time."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"what-happens-to-the-data-when-you-delete-things","h":"What happens to the data when you delete things","x":"What happens to the data when you delete things You delete The data The pod Safe. The replacement pod mounts the same claim. The Deployment or StatefulSet Safe. Claims are separate objects. The claim, with reclaim policy Delete (the usual default for dynamic volumes) Deleted for good , together with the volume. The claim, with reclaim policy Retain The volume is kept as Released and must be cleaned up or re-bound by hand. The namespace Deletes every claim in it. See above. A claim that a pod is still using is not removed immediately: it shows Terminating until the pod is gone."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"statefulsets","h":"StatefulSets","x":"StatefulSets A Deployment's pods are interchangeable: random names, any order, and (if you mount a claim) all sharing the same claim. That is wrong for most databases. A StatefulSet gives each pod an identity: Deployment StatefulSet Pod names db-7d9c5b8f6d-x2x9z (random) db-0 , db-1 (stable, ordered) Storage All replicas share the claims you reference volumeClaimTemplates : one claim per pod ( data-db-0 , data-db-1 ) Start and update order All at once / surge One at a time, in order Network identity Only through a Service Stable DNS name per pod via a headless Service ( db-0.db ) apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: StatefulSet metadata: name: db spec: serviceName: db # a headless Service (clusterIP: None) with this name replicas: 1 selector: matchLabels: {app: db} template: metadata: labels: {app: db} spec: containers: - name: db image: postgres:17 volumeMounts: - name: data mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data volumeClaimTemplates: - metadata: name: data spec: accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce] resources: requests: storage: 1Gi Two StatefulSet rules that matter in incidents volumeClaimTemplates can't be edited on an existing StatefulSet. A change requires deleting and recreating the StatefulSet (its claims and data stay). Deleting or scaling down a StatefulSet keeps its claims. That protects data, and it means a claim created with a wrong class survives and must be deleted by hand before a corrected template can create a new one."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"scenario-1-a-database-that-survives-its-pod","h":"Scenario 1: a database that survives its pod","x":"Scenario 1: a database that survives its pod This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"scenario-2-the-statefulset-that-never-gets-its-disk","h":"Scenario 2: the StatefulSet that never gets its disk","x":"Scenario 2: the StatefulSet that never gets its disk Follow the chain: pod → claim → class. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A claim requests storage; a StorageClass provisions a volume for it; the pod mounts the claim. A Pending pod with storage: describe the pod, then the claim, then check the classes. Waiting for first consumer is normal. Storageclass not found is a typo or a manifest from another cluster. Deleting a pod or workload keeps the data; deleting the claim (or the namespace) usually destroys it. StatefulSets give pods stable names and one claim each; their claim templates are immutable and their claims outlive them."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A new claim is Pending with the event \"waiting for first consumer to be created before binding\". What should you do? A. Delete and recreate the claim B. Nothing special: create the pod that uses it, and the volume will be provisioned C. Create a PersistentVolume by hand D. Change the access mode With WaitForFirstConsumer , provisioning is delayed until a pod using the claim is scheduled. It is the expected state before the pod exists. You delete a Deployment whose pod mounted the claim db-data . What happened to the data? A. It was deleted with the pod B. It was deleted with the Deployment C. It was moved to another node D. Nothing: the claim and its volume still exist Claims are independent objects. Only deleting the claim itself (or its namespace) removes the data under the usual Delete reclaim policy. db-0 of a StatefulSet is Pending because its claim names a StorageClass that doesn't exist. You correct the class in the manifest. Why is kubectl apply not enough? A. Claim templates are immutable and the old Pending claim still exists; both the StatefulSet and that claim must be deleted first B. StatefulSets can't use StorageClasses C. The pod must be deleted by hand first D. kubectl apply doesn't work on StatefulSets The API server forbids changing volumeClaimTemplates , and an existing claim is never re-created from the template."},{"l":18,"s":"18-storage","t":"Storage and Stateful Apps","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Persistent Volumes Storage Classes StatefulSets Dynamic volume provisioning"},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas On a shared cluster, some failures have nothing to do with your application: the cluster simply refuses what you ask, because of who you are or how much you have already used. These refusals are precise. This lesson shows how to read them and how to grant exactly what is missing. ⏱ 45 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Organise a cluster with namespaces , control access with RBAC , and understand how ResourceQuotas block the creation of pods. Why it matters: Forbidden errors and quota limits stop deployments in every company that shares clusters between teams. The quick \"fix\" is to grant admin rights; the correct fix takes the same time and does not create a security problem."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Work with namespaces and set a default namespace for kubectl. Explain subjects, Roles and RoleBindings, and create them. Take a Forbidden message apart into user, verb, resource and namespace. Test permissions with kubectl auth can-i and impersonation. Find out why a workload has fewer pods than replicas when a quota is involved."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"namespaces","h":"Namespaces","x":"Namespaces A namespace is a named partition of the cluster. Most objects live in exactly one: pods, Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, Secrets, claims. Names only have to be unique within a namespace, and permissions, quotas and network policies are attached per namespace. kubectl get namespaces kubectl create namespace team-a kubectl get pods -n team-a # one namespace kubectl get pods -A # all namespaces kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=team-a # change your default kubectl config view --minify | grep namespace # check what it is Namespaced Cluster-wide Pods, Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, Secrets, PVCs, Ingresses, Roles, RoleBindings, ServiceAccounts, NetworkPolicies, ResourceQuotas Nodes, Namespaces, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, IngressClasses, ClusterRoles, ClusterRoleBindings \"No resources found\" is often the wrong namespace Before concluding that something is missing, check which namespace you are looking at. kubectl get pods -A | grep NAME finds an object wherever it lives. And remember from earlier lessons: a pod can only reference ConfigMaps, Secrets and claims in its own namespace, and short Service names resolve within the caller's namespace. Deleting a namespace deletes everything in it kubectl delete namespace team-a removes all its workloads, Secrets and claims, and with them, usually, the data on their volumes."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"rbac-who-may-do-what","h":"RBAC: who may do what","x":"RBAC: who may do what Every request to the API server comes from an identity: a human user , or a ServiceAccount , which is the identity pods and automation use. Role-based access control decides whether the request is allowed. Nothing is allowed unless a rule grants it. subject RoleBinding Role (User, Group or ───── \"these subjects get ───────► \"may get, list, patch ServiceAccount) this role, in THIS deployments\" namespace\" Object Scope Contains Role One namespace Rules: verbs on resources ClusterRole Cluster-wide (or reusable in any namespace) Rules RoleBinding One namespace Subjects + a reference to a Role or ClusterRole ClusterRoleBinding All namespaces Subjects + a ClusterRole apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata: name: deploy-manager namespace: team-a rules: - apiGroups: [\"apps\"] # \"\" is the core group: pods, services, secrets… resources: [\"deployments\"] verbs: [\"get\", \"list\", \"watch\", \"create\", \"update\", \"patch\"] --- apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: RoleBinding metadata: name: deployer-binding namespace: team-a subjects: - kind: ServiceAccount name: deployer namespace: team-a roleRef: apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io kind: Role name: deploy-manager kubectl create serviceaccount deployer kubectl create role deploy-manager --verb=get,list,watch,create,update,patch --resource=deployments kubectl create rolebinding deployer-binding --role=deploy-manager --serviceaccount=team-a:deployer Verbs kubectl command Verb(s) needed get NAME / get (list) / -w get / list / watch create , first apply create apply on an existing object, patch , scale , set image , rollout restart , rollout undo patch (plus get ) edit , replace update delete delete logs / exec get on pods/log / create on pods/exec Reading Forbidden Error from server (Forbidden): deployments.apps \"web\" is forbidden: User \"system:serviceaccount:shop:ci\" cannot patch resource \"deployments\" in API group \"apps\" in the namespace \"shop\" The message is a complete work order: Part Value here Goes into Who system:serviceaccount:shop:ci = ServiceAccount ci in namespace shop The binding's subjects Verb patch The role's verbs Resource deployments The role's resources API group apps The role's apiGroups Where namespace shop The namespace of the Role and RoleBinding kubectl auth can-i patch deployments -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:ci # yes / no kubectl auth can-i --list -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:ci # everything it may do kubectl get rolebindings -n shop -o wide # who is bound to what ❌ \"Just make it work\" kubectl create clusterrolebinding ci-admin \\ --clusterrole=cluster-admin \\ --serviceaccount=shop:ci The pipeline can now read every Secret and delete every namespace in the cluster. A leaked CI token becomes a full compromise. ✅ Least privilege Add the one missing verb to the existing Role, in the one namespace, and verify with auth can-i . It takes the same thirty seconds."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"resourcequotas","h":"ResourceQuotas","x":"ResourceQuotas A ResourceQuota caps what a namespace may consume in total: number of pods, sum of CPU and memory requests and limits, number of Services or claims. apiVersion: v1 kind: ResourceQuota metadata: name: shop-quota namespace: shop spec: hard: pods: \"8\" requests.cpu: \"2\" requests.memory: 2Gi kubectl describe resourcequota -n shop # Used versus Hard A quota is enforced when an object is created : the API server refuses a pod that would exceed it. The consequence is a symptom that looks unlike anything so far: NAME READY UP-TO-DATE AVAILABLE AGE web 2/4 2 2 10m NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE web-lz4hfd75pd-l2r7l 1/1 Running 0 10m web-lz4hfd75pd-ljbzj 1/1 Running 0 10m Two pods are missing, and there is no Pending or failing pod to describe, because the missing pods were never stored. The refusal was reported to whoever tried to create them: the ReplicaSet . Warning FailedCreate 15s replicaset-controller Error creating: pods \"web-lz4hfd75pd-j6fdt\" is forbidden: exceeded quota: shop-quota, requested: pods=1, used: pods=4, limited: pods=4 Go up the ownership chain When a Deployment has fewer pods than it should and no pod looks unhealthy, look one level up: kubectl describe replicaset , or kubectl get events for the namespace. The same applies to Jobs and StatefulSets. A second quota effect: if a quota covers requests.cpu or requests.memory , every pod must declare those requests, or it is rejected with must specify requests.cpu ."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"scenario-1-a-namespace-and-a-least-privilege-service-account","h":"Scenario 1: a namespace and a least-privilege service account","x":"Scenario 1: a namespace and a least-privilege service account This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"scenario-2-forbidden","h":"Scenario 2: Forbidden","x":"Scenario 2: Forbidden This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"scenario-3-scaled-to-4-stuck-at-2-and-nothing-is-pending","h":"Scenario 3: scaled to 4, stuck at 2, and nothing is Pending","x":"Scenario 3: scaled to 4, stuck at 2, and nothing is Pending This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Namespaces partition names, permissions, quotas and policies. Check your namespace before anything else. RBAC = subject + Role (verbs on resources) + RoleBinding, per namespace. Nothing is allowed by default. A Forbidden message names user, verb, resource, group and namespace: grant exactly that. kubectl auth can-i … --as=… tests permissions without switching identity. Quotas reject pods at creation. Missing pods with no Pending pod: read the ReplicaSet's events."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A ServiceAccount may get and list Deployments. Which command will it be refused? A. kubectl get deployments B. kubectl get deployment web -o yaml C. kubectl rollout restart deployment/web D. kubectl describe deployment web A rollout restart patches the Deployment's pod template, which needs the patch verb. What does a RoleBinding in namespace shop that references the ClusterRole view grant? A. Read access to the resources view covers, in namespace shop only B. Read access in every namespace C. Nothing; a RoleBinding can't reference a ClusterRole D. Admin access in shop A RoleBinding limits whatever role it references to its own namespace. This is how one ClusterRole is reused across namespaces. A Deployment wants 6 replicas and has 3. No pod is Pending or failing. Where do you look? A. The node's kubelet logs B. The ReplicaSet's events ( kubectl describe rs ) for FailedCreate, for example a quota C. The container logs of the running pods D. The Service's endpoints Pods that were rejected at admission never exist. The controller that tried to create them records the reason."},{"l":19,"s":"19-namespaces-rbac-quotas","t":"Namespaces, RBAC and Quotas","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Namespaces Using RBAC authorization Resource Quotas RBAC good practices"},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values By now an application is half a dozen YAML files, and each environment needs slightly different numbers in them. Helm packages those files with their variables, installs them as one unit, and remembers every version it installed. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand charts , releases and values , and install, inspect and upgrade an application with Helm. Why it matters: most third-party software for Kubernetes is distributed as a Helm chart, and most teams deploy their own services with one. To debug a Helm-managed application you need to know what Helm did, what it stored, and what it does not check."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain what a chart, a release and a revision are. Add a chart repository and search it. Install and upgrade a release, overriding values. Inspect what Helm rendered and applied. Explain what \"STATUS: deployed\" does and does not mean."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The front end consists of a Deployment, a Service and an optional Ingress. Development wants one replica, production wants three and an Ingress. Copying the manifests per environment and editing them by hand is how the two drift apart."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"three-words","h":"Three words","x":"Three words Term Meaning Analogy Chart A package: templated manifests, default values, metadata. Has a version. An installer Release One installation of a chart, with a name, in a namespace. An installed program Revision One version of a release. Each install, upgrade and rollback creates a new one. An entry in the update history chart templates + values (defaults ◄ your overrides) │ ▼ render plain manifests ──► applied to the cluster ──► revision record stored as a Secret Helm 4 has no server-side component. The helm command renders templates on your machine, talks to the Kubernetes API with your kubectl credentials, and keeps its bookkeeping in Secrets inside the release's namespace."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"repositories","h":"Repositories","x":"Repositories helm repo add shop https://charts.shop.example # give a repository a local name helm repo update # refresh the list of charts helm search repo shop # newest version of each chart helm search repo shop/web --versions # all versions NAME CHART VERSION APP VERSION DESCRIPTION shop/api 1.1.0 1.1 The shop REST API shop/db 1.0.0 17 A single-instance PostgreSQL for the shop (StatefulSet with a persistent volume) shop/web 2.0.0 2.0 The shop web front end Two version numbers appear, and they are independent: the chart version is the version of the packaging, the app version is the version of the software inside (usually the default image tag)."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"values","h":"Values","x":"Values A chart exposes its settings as values , with defaults in its values.yaml . Always read them before installing: helm show values shop/web replicaCount: 2 image: repository: shop/web tag: \"\" # defaults to the chart's appVersion env: API_URL: http://api:3000 service: type: ClusterIP port: 80 ingress: enabled: false host: shop.local You override values on the command line or, better, in a file of your own: helm install web shop/web --set replicaCount=1 --set env.BANNER=\"Hello\" helm install web shop/web -f prod-values.yaml # preferred: a file you can review and commit Precedence, lowest to highest: the chart's values.yaml , then each -f file in order, then --set ."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"install-and-inspect","h":"Install and inspect","x":"Install and inspect helm install web shop/web --version 1.0.0 -n shop --set replicaCount=1 NAME: web LAST DEPLOYED: Sun Oct 11 09:00:41 2026 NAMESPACE: shop STATUS: deployed REVISION: 1 DESCRIPTION: Install complete TEST SUITE: None NOTES: web 1.0 was installed as release \"web\" in namespace \"shop\". ... helm list -n shop # releases in a namespace (-A for all namespaces) helm status web -n shop helm get values web -n shop # the overrides you supplied (--all: merged with defaults) helm get manifest web -n shop # the exact YAML Helm applied helm history web -n shop # all revisions kubectl -n shop get all # the objects are ordinary Kubernetes objects \"deployed\" means \"accepted\", not \"running\" By default Helm reports success as soon as the API server has accepted the manifests. It does not wait for pods. A chart whose image crashes on start, or whose pods are Pending, still shows STATUS: deployed . Either check with kubectl afterwards, or add --wait , which makes Helm wait until the workloads are ready and fail (after --timeout , five minutes by default) if they are not. Releases are namespaced A release lives in one namespace, and every Helm command needs the same -n . helm list without it shows only the current namespace, which leads to \"my release has disappeared\" when it is simply somewhere else. helm list -A shows them all."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"upgrade","h":"Upgrade","x":"Upgrade helm upgrade web shop/web --version 1.1.0 -n shop --set replicaCount=3 --wait helm history web -n shop REVISION UPDATED STATUS CHART APP VERSION DESCRIPTION 1 Sun Oct 11 09:00:41 2026 superseded web-1.0.0 1.0 Install complete 2 Sun Oct 11 09:03:10 2026 deployed web-1.1.0 1.1 Upgrade complete helm upgrade renders the new chart version with the values you give, compares the result with the previous revision's manifest, and applies the differences. The Deployment's pod template changed, so Kubernetes performs an ordinary rolling update (Lesson 13). Helm did not replace Kubernetes' mechanics; it only produced the new manifests. One command for both cases helm upgrade --install web shop/web -f values.yaml -n shop installs the release if it does not exist and upgrades it if it does. That makes it the natural command for pipelines."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"what-helm-owns","h":"What Helm owns","x":"What Helm owns Helm labels the objects it creates ( app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm ) and records their release in annotations. Two consequences: If an object with the same name already exists and was not created by this release, the install fails with … exists and cannot be imported into the current release: invalid ownership metadata . Helm refuses to take over foreign objects. A manual kubectl edit on a Helm-managed object works, and is overwritten by the next helm upgrade . Change the values instead."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"scenario-install-inspect-and-upgrade-a-release","h":"Scenario: install, inspect and upgrade a release","x":"Scenario: install, inspect and upgrade a release This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Chart = package, release = an installation of it, revision = one version of that installation. Values configure a chart: defaults ◄ -f files ◄ --set . Read helm show values first. helm get manifest and helm get values show exactly what was applied and with which settings. STATUS: deployed only means the manifests were accepted. Use --wait or check the pods. Releases are per namespace; Helm stores its history in Secrets there."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz helm install printed STATUS: deployed , but the application doesn't respond. What is the first thing to check? A. Reinstall the chart B. The pods: without --wait , Helm doesn't verify that they started C. The chart repository's availability D. The Helm version Helm's default success criterion is that the API server accepted the manifests. From there on it is ordinary Kubernetes debugging. You pass -f prod.yaml --set replicaCount=5 , the chart's default is 2 and prod.yaml says 3. How many replicas result? A. 2 B. 3 C. 5 D. An error, because the values conflict --set has the highest precedence, then -f files, then the chart's defaults. helm list shows nothing, although a colleague installed the release yesterday. What is the most likely explanation? A. The release is in another namespace; try helm list -A B. Helm deleted it overnight C. The cluster was reset D. Releases are only visible to the user who installed them Releases and their history are stored per namespace, and helm list looks only at the current one unless told otherwise."},{"l":20,"s":"20-helm-basics","t":"Helm Basics: Charts, Releases, Values","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Using Helm Charts Values files helm upgrade"},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Writing and Debugging Charts Sooner or later a chart fails to render, and the error mentions a line number, an angle-bracketed expression and \"nil pointer\". This lesson opens the chart up: how templates turn values into YAML, and how to find out which of the two stages went wrong. ⏱ 50 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Read and write Helm templates , and debug charts with helm lint and helm template . Why it matters: template errors happen before anything reaches the cluster, so no Kubernetes tool can help you. The fix is quick once you know that rendering has two stages and which message belongs to which."},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Describe the layout of a chart. Use values, built-in objects, pipelines, conditionals and loops in templates. Control whitespace and indentation with {{- and nindent . Tell a template error from a YAML parse error and locate each. Render a chart locally to inspect its output."},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"the-layout-of-a-chart","h":"The layout of a chart","x":"The layout of a chart web/ ├── Chart.yaml name, version, appVersion ├── values.yaml default values └── templates/ ├── _helpers.tpl named snippets (\"define\"), not rendered by itself ├── deployment.yaml ┐ ├── service.yaml │ templates: YAML with {{ ... }} placeholders ├── ingress.yaml ┘ └── NOTES.txt text shown after install helm create hello # scaffold a new chart helm lint hello # static checks, including a test render helm template demo hello # render locally and print the manifests; nothing is installed"},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"templates-in-five-steps","h":"Templates in five steps","x":"Templates in five steps 1. Values and built-in objects metadata: name: {{ .Release.Name }} spec: replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }} template: spec: containers: - name: {{ .Chart.Name }} image: \"{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion }}\" Object Contains .Values The merged values: chart defaults, your files, --set .Release .Name , .Namespace , .Revision , .IsInstall , .IsUpgrade .Chart Fields of Chart.yaml , capitalised: .Name , .Version , .AppVersion 2. Pipelines and functions A value can be passed through functions with | , like a shell pipe: Expression Effect {{ .Values.name | quote }} Wraps the value in double quotes. Essential for strings that look like numbers or booleans. {{ .Values.tag | default \"latest\" }} Uses the fallback when the value is empty. {{ required \"image.tag is required\" .Values.image.tag }} Aborts rendering with your message when the value is missing. {{ toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 12 }} Dumps a whole structure as YAML, starting on a new line, indented 12 spaces. {{ include \"web.labels\" . | nindent 4 }} Inserts a named snippet from _helpers.tpl . 3. Conditionals and loops {{- if .Values.ingress.enabled }} apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress # ... {{- end }} env: {{- range $key, $value := .Values.env }} - name: {{ $key }} value: {{ $value | quote }} {{- end }} 4. Whitespace A template action leaves its own line behind as an empty line. A dash inside the braces removes whitespace on that side: {{- eats everything before the action up to the previous text, including the line break; -}} does the same after it. That is why control lines are written {{- if … }} and {{- end }} . 5. Indentation is your job Helm inserts text; it knows nothing about YAML structure. Whatever a template produces must be valid YAML at the place where it lands. For multi-line blocks, nindent N is the tool: it starts a new line and indents every line by N spaces. ❌ Breaks as soon as the value has two lines resources: {{ toYaml .Values.resources }} Only the first line gets the 12 spaces written in the template. The second line starts at column 0. ✅ Every line indented resources: {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 12 }} {{- removes the template's own indentation; nindent 12 adds a line break and indents all lines."},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"two-stages-two-kinds-of-error","h":"Two stages, two kinds of error","x":"Two stages, two kinds of error templates + values ──(1) evaluate Go templates──► text ──(2) parse as YAML──► manifests fails with \"template: …\" fails with \"YAML parse error on …\" Stage 1: template errors Error: template: web/templates/service.yaml:11:23: executing \"web/templates/service.yaml\" at <.Values.service.ports.http>: nil pointer evaluating interface {}.http Part of the message Meaning web/templates/service.yaml:11:23 File, line and column in the template source . at <.Values.service.ports.http> The expression that failed. nil pointer evaluating interface {}.http Something before .http does not exist: .Values.service.ports is undefined, so it has no field http . The fix is always the same comparison: open values.yaml and walk the path in the expression key by key. Typical causes are a typo, a renamed value, or a nested key that only exists when an optional section is filled in. Other stage-1 messages Cause function \"foo\" not defined A misspelt function name. unexpected EOF / unexpected {{end}} An if , range or with without its end , or one end too many. error calling include: … no template \"web.lables\" A misspelt name of a snippet defined in _helpers.tpl . error calling required: … The chart author demands a value you did not set. The message says which. A missing leaf value is not an error {{ .Values.image.tagg }} (a typo in the last key) renders as an empty string without complaint, because looking up a missing key in an existing map is allowed. The result is an image like shop/web: and a pod in InvalidImageName . Charts guard important values with required or default for this reason. Stage 2: YAML errors Error: YAML parse error on web/templates/deployment.yaml: error converting YAML to JSON: yaml: line 33: did not find expected '-' indicator The templates ran, and their output is not valid YAML. Here the line number refers to the rendered text , not to the template file, so opening the template at line 33 misleads you. Look at what was produced: helm template web ./web --debug # prints the rendered output even when it is invalid env: - name: PORT value: \"8080\" - name: API_URL # ← two spaces too far left: not part of the list above value: \"http://api:3000\""},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"a-chart-debugging-routine","h":"A chart-debugging routine","x":"A chart-debugging routine helm lint CHART : reports the first problem of either stage, with the file. Template error? Compare the quoted expression with values.yaml . YAML error? helm template … --debug and look at the rendered text near the line. Renders, but looks wrong? helm template NAME CHART -f my-values.yaml -s templates/deployment.yaml shows one file with your values. Before a real upgrade: helm upgrade … --dry-run renders against the cluster and shows what would be applied."},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"scenario-1-create-a-chart-and-drive-it-with-values","h":"Scenario 1: create a chart and drive it with values","x":"Scenario 1: create a chart and drive it with values This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"scenario-2-the-chart-that-won-t-render","h":"Scenario 2: the chart that won't render","x":"Scenario 2: the chart that won't render Two mistakes, one per stage. Let helm lint tell you which stage you are in. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A chart is templates plus default values. .Values , .Release and .Chart feed the templates. Helm produces text; correct YAML indentation is the template's responsibility. Use nindent for blocks. Stage 1, \"template: file:line … at <expr>\": the expression is wrong; compare it with the values. Stage 2, \"YAML parse error … line N\": the output is broken; the line refers to the rendered text. Use helm template --debug . helm lint and helm template need no cluster. Run them in CI."},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Rendering fails with at <.Values.database.host>: nil pointer evaluating interface {}.host . What is wrong? A. host is an empty string B. The cluster is unreachable C. The YAML indentation is wrong D. .Values.database doesn't exist, so .host can't be read from it The message names the field that could not be evaluated; the problem is its parent. Check whether database: is defined in the values. Helm reports YAML parse error on web/templates/deployment.yaml: … line 40 . The template file has 28 lines. Why? A. The line number refers to the rendered output, which is longer than the template B. Helm counts blank lines twice C. The error is in values.yaml D. The chart contains two files with the same name Loops, includes and toYaml expand to many lines. Inspect the rendered text with helm template --debug . What does {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 10 }} do? A. Indents only the first line by 10 spaces B. Removes the whitespace before the action, then inserts the YAML on a new line with every line indented 10 spaces C. Converts the YAML to JSON D. Validates the values against a schema {{- trims preceding whitespace including the newline; nindent supplies a fresh newline and consistent indentation for all lines."},{"l":21,"s":"21-writing-charts","t":"Writing and Debugging Charts","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Chart template guide Flow control and whitespace Debugging templates Template function list"},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases Installing a chart is the easy part. Releases live for years and are upgraded hundreds of times, and that is where values get lost, upgrades fail half-way, and a release ends up in a state where Helm refuses to touch it. This lesson covers each of those and the way out. ⏱ 45 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Upgrade releases without losing settings, use --wait and automatic rollback, read the release history , and repair failed and pending releases. Why it matters: a Helm release has its own state, separate from the state of the pods. When the two disagree (Helm says failed , the app runs; or Helm says deployed , the app is down; or Helm says another operation is in progress and nothing is running), you need to understand Helm's bookkeeping to proceed."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Predict which values an upgrade will use. Make Helm verify a rollout and undo it automatically on failure. Interpret every status in helm history . Roll back a release. Recover a release stuck in pending-upgrade ."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"which-values-does-an-upgrade-use","h":"Which values does an upgrade use?","x":"Which values does an upgrade use? This is the most common Helm surprise. An upgrade computes the values from scratch : You run Values used helm upgrade web shop/web (no value flags at all) The previous revision's overrides are reused. helm upgrade web shop/web --set image.tag=1.1 Only chart defaults plus image.tag . Earlier overrides are dropped. … --reuse-values --set image.tag=1.1 Previous overrides, plus image.tag . … -f values.yaml Chart defaults plus exactly what the file says. … --reset-values Chart defaults only. ❌ The silent regression helm install web shop/web --set replicaCount=3 # three weeks later helm upgrade web shop/web --set image.tag=1.1 # replicaCount is back to the chart default (2) ✅ Values in a file, passed every time # values-prod.yaml is in git and contains ALL overrides helm upgrade --install web shop/web \\ -f values-prod.yaml --wait What is deployed is always \"the chart plus this file\". No history to remember. helm get values web -n shop # what the current revision was given helm get values web -n shop --all # merged with the chart's defaults --reuse-values and new chart versions --reuse-values re-applies the old overrides on top of the new chart's defaults. That is usually what you want, but if the new chart renamed or restructured a value, your old override silently no longer applies. Read the chart's release notes on upgrades, and prefer a values file."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"making-helm-check-its-own-work","h":"Making Helm check its own work","x":"Making Helm check its own work Flag Effect --wait Wait until Deployments, StatefulSets and so on are ready. On timeout the command fails and the revision is marked failed . --timeout 3m How long --wait waits (default 5m). --rollback-on-failure Implies waiting. If the upgrade fails, Helm rolls back to the previous revision by itself. (Called --atomic before Helm 4; the old name still works with a deprecation warning.) --dry-run Render and validate, change nothing. $ helm upgrade web shop/web --version 1.2.0 -n shop --wait --timeout 20s Error: UPGRADE FAILED: context deadline exceeded Context deadline exceeded is Helm's way of saying \"I waited for the timeout and the workloads were still not ready\". Note what happened in the cluster: Kubernetes started a rolling update, the new pod crashed, and the old pods kept serving (Lesson 13). Helm noticed only because of --wait . Without it the release would say deployed while the rollout is stuck."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"reading-the-history","h":"Reading the history","x":"Reading the history helm history web -n shop REVISION UPDATED STATUS CHART APP VERSION DESCRIPTION 1 Sun Oct 11 09:00:40 2026 deployed web-1.0.0 1.0 Install complete 2 Sun Oct 11 09:01:20 2026 failed web-1.2.0 1.2 Upgrade \"web\" failed: context deadline exceeded 3 Sun Oct 11 09:11:40 2026 pending-upgrade web-1.2.0 1.2 Preparing upgrade Status Meaning deployed The current, successfully applied revision. There is at most one. superseded An earlier revision that was replaced by a later successful one. failed The operation ended with an error. The cluster may be partly changed. pending-install / pending-upgrade / pending-rollback An operation started and has not recorded its end. uninstalled Removed, with history kept ( --keep-history )."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"rollback","h":"Rollback","x":"Rollback helm rollback web 1 -n shop # to a specific revision; omit the number for \"the previous one\" helm history web -n shop A rollback re-applies the stored manifest of the chosen revision and records that as a new revision (\"Rollback to 1\"). History is never rewritten. As with kubectl rollout undo , the cluster then performs a normal rolling update back to the old pod template."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"another-operation-is-in-progress","h":"\"another operation is in progress\"","x":"\"another operation is in progress\" Helm writes a revision record with a pending status before it starts changing the cluster, and updates it when it finishes. If the Helm process dies in between (a cancelled CI job, a closed laptop, a killed pod), the record stays pending forever. Helm treats a pending record as a lock: $ helm upgrade web shop/web --version 2.0.0 -n shop Error: UPGRADE FAILED: another operation (install/upgrade/rollback) is in progress No operation is in progress; only the record says so. Two more things make this confusing: helm list hides releases whose latest revision is pending (use helm list -a ), and the application may be running perfectly well the whole time. Step Command 1. Make the release visible helm list -n shop -a 2. Find the last good revision helm history web -n shop 3. Roll back to it. This marks the stuck revision as failed and re-applies known-good manifests. helm rollback web 1 -n shop 4. Run the upgrade again, this time with --wait helm upgrade web shop/web --version 2.0.0 -n shop --wait Don't reach for uninstall helm uninstall also \"fixes\" a stuck release, by deleting the application and, depending on the chart, its volume claims. In production that is an outage and possibly data loss. Rollback first. Where Helm keeps this state Each revision is a Secret named sh.helm.release.v1.NAME.vN in the release's namespace ( kubectl -n shop get secrets ). Its status label is what helm history shows. Knowing that these are just Secrets explains why Helm needs no server, and why deleting the namespace erases the release history too."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"scenario-1-upgrade-without-losing-your-settings-then-roll-back","h":"Scenario 1: upgrade without losing your settings, then roll back","x":"Scenario 1: upgrade without losing your settings, then roll back This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"scenario-2-another-operation-is-in-progress","h":"Scenario 2: another operation is in progress","x":"Scenario 2: another operation is in progress This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Passing any --set or -f without --reuse-values drops earlier overrides. Keep all overrides in a values file and pass it on every upgrade. --wait makes Helm verify readiness; --rollback-on-failure also undoes a failed upgrade. helm history is the release's own state: deployed, superseded, failed, pending. A pending revision left by a killed Helm process blocks further operations. helm list -a shows it; helm rollback clears it. A rollback creates a new revision from an old manifest. Uninstall is not a repair tool."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A release was installed with --set replicaCount=5 . Later someone runs helm upgrade web shop/web --set image.tag=2.0 . How many replicas does the release have now? A. 5 B. 0 C. The chart's default D. The upgrade fails Giving a value flag without --reuse-values recomputes the values from the chart defaults plus only the new flags. helm upgrade fails with \"another operation (install/upgrade/rollback) is in progress\", and helm list shows no release. What is the state? A. The latest revision is stuck in a pending status; helm list -a shows it and a rollback clears it B. Another user is upgrading right now; wait C. The release was uninstalled D. The cluster is out of capacity A killed Helm process leaves a pending record that acts as a lock. Nothing is actually running. What does helm rollback web 2 do to the history? A. Deletes all revisions after 2 B. Changes revision 2's status back to deployed C. Nothing; rollbacks are not recorded D. Adds a new revision whose content equals revision 2 History is append-only. The rollback is recorded as the newest revision, described as \"Rollback to 2\"."},{"l":22,"s":"22-upgrades-and-rollbacks","t":"Upgrades, Rollbacks and Broken Releases","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading helm upgrade helm rollback helm history Storage backends: where releases are recorded"},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application So far, you ran kubectl apply and helm upgrade . With GitOps, nobody does. The desired state lives in a git repository, and a controller in the cluster keeps the cluster equal to it. Argo CD is the most widely used such controller. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand the GitOps model, and create, sync and inspect an Argo CD Application . Why it matters: in a GitOps setup, the two questions you ask about every deployment problem are new: \"does the cluster match git?\" and \"is what is in git actually working?\". Argo CD answers them with two separate statuses, and reading those correctly is most of the skill."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain GitOps and how it differs from running kubectl in a pipeline. Describe an Application's source and destination. Distinguish sync status from health status . Create and synchronise an application with the argocd CLI. Find the Application object with kubectl."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation Three people deploy the shop, each with their own kubeconfig and their own habits. Nobody can say with certainty what is running in production, or who changed the replica count last Tuesday. The team decides: from now on, production is whatever is on the main branch of the gitops repository."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"what-gitops-means","h":"What GitOps means","x":"What GitOps means Push: pipeline runs kubectl/helm Pull: GitOps Who changes the cluster CI jobs and people, from outside A controller inside the cluster Source of truth Whatever was applied last The git repository Manual change in the cluster Stays, unnoticed, until something overwrites it Detected as drift; optionally reverted Audit trail Pipeline logs, if kept Git history: who, what, when, why, reviewed by whom Rollback Re-run an old pipeline, hopefully git revert Cluster credentials Stored in the CI system Never leave the cluster It is the reconciliation idea from Lesson 9, one level up: Kubernetes makes pods match Deployments; Argo CD makes Deployments match git."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"argo-cd-in-the-cluster","h":"Argo CD in the cluster","x":"Argo CD in the cluster Argo CD is itself a set of pods, by convention in the namespace argocd : kubectl -n argocd get pods Component Job argocd-repo-server Fetches repositories and generates manifests from them (plain YAML, Helm, Kustomize). argocd-application-controller Compares generated manifests with the live cluster and performs syncs. argocd-server The API and web UI that the argocd CLI talks to. In the sandbox the CLI is already logged in. On a real installation you would first run argocd login HOST ."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"the-application","h":"The Application","x":"The Application An Application connects a place in git to a place in a cluster. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Application metadata: name: shop namespace: argocd spec: project: default source: # WHERE the desired state is repoURL: https://git.shop.example/shop/gitops.git targetRevision: HEAD # a branch, tag or commit path: apps # directory inside the repository directory: recurse: true destination: # WHERE it should be applied server: https://kubernetes.default.svc # \"the cluster Argo CD runs in\" namespace: shop syncPolicy: syncOptions: - CreateNamespace=true The same thing with the CLI: argocd app create shop \\ --repo https://git.shop.example/shop/gitops.git \\ --path apps --directory-recurse \\ --dest-server https://kubernetes.default.svc \\ --dest-namespace shop \\ --sync-option CreateNamespace=true An Application is just a Kubernetes object kubectl -n argocd get applications lists them. That means Applications can themselves be stored in git and managed by another Application (the \"app of apps\" pattern), so even the list of what is deployed is under version control."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"two-statuses","h":"Two statuses","x":"Two statuses argocd app list argocd app get shop Name: argocd/shop Project: default Server: https://kubernetes.default.svc Namespace: shop Source: - Repo: https://git.shop.example/shop/gitops.git Target: HEAD Path: apps Sync Policy: Manual Sync Status: OutOfSync from HEAD (ba66957) Health Status: Missing GROUP KIND NAMESPACE NAME STATUS HEALTH HOOK MESSAGE apps Deployment shop api OutOfSync Missing Service shop api OutOfSync Missing apps Deployment shop web OutOfSync Missing Service shop web OutOfSync Missing Sync status Question: does the cluster match git? Synced Every resource in git exists in the cluster with the declared content. OutOfSync Something differs: git has a change not yet applied, or the cluster was changed by hand. Unknown Argo CD could not determine the desired state at all (Lesson 25). Health status Question: is it actually working? Healthy All resources are fine: Deployments fully rolled out, and so on. Progressing Not healthy yet, but may become so: a rollout is under way, pods are starting. Degraded Something failed: a Deployment exceeded its progress deadline, a Job failed. Missing A resource declared in git does not exist in the cluster. The two are independent Synced and Degraded is common and important: the cluster matches git exactly, and what is in git does not work. OutOfSync and Healthy is just as possible: everything runs, but someone changed something by hand. Always read both."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"sync","h":"Sync","x":"Sync argocd app sync shop # apply what is in git argocd app wait shop --health # block until it is healthy (or time out) kubectl -n shop get pods A sync is, at its core, a kubectl apply of the generated manifests, performed by the controller. Each resource then reports its own result: Operation: Sync Sync Revision: ba6695702e37edf552b1f0cdb0cca3fa4f38b355 Phase: Succeeded Message: successfully synced (all tasks run) GROUP KIND NAMESPACE NAME STATUS HEALTH HOOK MESSAGE Service shop web Synced Healthy service/web created apps Deployment shop web Synced Progressing deployment.apps/web created Right after the sync the application is Synced and Progressing : the Deployments exist, the pods are still starting. A minute later it is Healthy . With manual sync policy, which is the default, Argo CD only applies changes when you ask. The next lesson switches to automated sync. How quickly does Argo CD notice a commit? It polls each repository every few minutes. To make it look immediately, refresh the application: argocd app get shop --refresh . In production, a webhook from the git server triggers the refresh the moment a commit is pushed."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"scenario-your-first-argo-cd-application","h":"Scenario: your first Argo CD Application","x":"Scenario: your first Argo CD Application This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways GitOps: git holds the desired state; a controller in the cluster makes the cluster match it. An Application = source (repo, revision, path) + destination (cluster, namespace). Sync status answers \"cluster equals git?\"; health status answers \"does it work?\". They are independent. A sync applies the generated manifests; health follows once the workloads are ready. Applications are Kubernetes objects in the argocd namespace."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz An application is Synced and Degraded . What does that mean? A. Argo CD can't reach the git repository B. The cluster matches git, but the deployed resources are not working C. Someone changed the cluster by hand D. The sync is still running Sync status only compares declarations. Health looks at whether the resources function. Here the fault is in what git declares, or in something the manifests depend on. What does an Application's destination specify? A. The git branch to read B. The directory inside the repository C. The container registry D. The cluster and namespace to deploy to Source says where the desired state comes from; destination says where it is applied. With the default (manual) sync policy, a new commit is pushed to the repository. What does Argo CD do? A. Detects it on its next refresh and shows the application as OutOfSync, without applying it B. Applies it immediately C. Deletes the application D. Nothing; it never looks at git again after creation Manual policy means Argo CD reports the difference and waits for argocd app sync ."},{"l":23,"s":"23-gitops-with-argocd","t":"GitOps with Argo CD: Your First Application","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Argo CD core concepts Getting started Declarative setup: Applications as manifests OpenGitOps principles"},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git With manual sync, Argo CD is a reporting tool. Turn on automated sync, pruning and self-healing, and git becomes the only way to change production. That is powerful, and it changes how you fix things at three in the morning. ⏱ 45 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Configure automated sync , prune and self-heal , deploy and roll back through git , and investigate drift . Why it matters: on a self-healing application, a kubectl hot-fix disappears within seconds. If you don't know why, you will fight the system. If you do, you will fix the problem in the one place where the fix lasts."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain what automated sync, prune and self-heal each do. Deploy a change by committing to git. Use argocd app diff to see exactly what differs. Roll back with git revert , and say when argocd app rollback applies. Tell a pending commit from drift."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"the-situation","h":"The situation","x":"The situation The shop is managed by Argo CD. The team wants deployments to happen when a pull request is merged, wants deleted manifests to disappear from the cluster, and wants to stop finding surprises left behind by late-night kubectl sessions."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"three-switches","h":"Three switches","x":"Three switches Setting Off (default) On Automated sync New commits show as OutOfSync until someone runs argocd app sync . New commits are applied automatically. Prune A resource removed from git stays in the cluster, shown as OutOfSync (\"requires pruning\"). Resources removed from git are deleted from the cluster. Self-heal A manual change in the cluster shows as OutOfSync and stays. Manual changes are reverted to the git state within seconds. argocd app set shop --sync-policy automated --auto-prune --self-heal argocd app get shop # Sync Policy: Automated (Prune) spec: syncPolicy: automated: prune: true selfHeal: true syncOptions: - CreateNamespace=true Prune deletes With prune enabled, deleting a file from the repository (or moving it outside the application's path, or a rendering change that drops a resource) deletes the object from the cluster, including a StatefulSet or a Namespace. Review deletions in pull requests as carefully as additions. For objects that must never be pruned there is the annotation argocd.argoproj.io/sync-options: Prune=false ."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"deploying-is-committing","h":"Deploying is committing","x":"Deploying is committing git clone https://git.shop.example/shop/gitops.git cd gitops sed -i 's/replicas: 2/replicas: 3/' apps/web/deployment.yaml git diff # review your own change git add . git commit -m \"web: scale to 3 replicas\" git push argocd app get shop --refresh # don't wait for the next poll kubectl -n shop get deployment web Nothing else is needed. In a real team the push would be a pull request, reviewed and merged by someone else, and that review is the approval step for production. argocd app history shop ID DATE REVISION 0 2026-10-11 09:00:02 +0000 UTC HEAD (ba66957) 1 2026-10-11 09:01:22 +0000 UTC HEAD (d6b6dac)"},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"drift","h":"Drift","x":"Drift Drift is any difference between the live cluster and git that did not come from git. Somebody ran kubectl scale , kubectl set image or kubectl edit . kubectl -n shop scale deployment web --replicas=1 # someone \"just tries something\" argocd app diff shop ===== apps/Deployment shop/web ====== 10c10 < replicas: 1 --- > replicas: 3 Policy What happens next Manual sync The app stays OutOfSync . The change remains until someone syncs. Automated, no self-heal Still OutOfSync : automated sync reacts to new commits , not to drift. Automated with self-heal After a few seconds Argo CD re-applies git. The Deployment is back at 3. OutOfSync: which side moved? Clue Git moved (pending change) Cluster moved (drift) Revision in argocd app get A new commit hash Same commit as the last sync argocd app history Last entry is older than the newest commit Last entry is the current commit git log A recent commit touching that file Nothing recent In both cases argocd app diff shows the exact fields, and argocd app sync makes the cluster equal to git."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"rolling-back","h":"Rolling back","x":"Rolling back ❌ Fighting the controller kubectl -n shop set image deploy/web web=shop/web:1.0 # ...ten seconds later it is 1.2 again With self-heal on, every manual correction is drift, and drift is reverted. The cluster is faithfully reproducing a mistake that lives in git. ✅ Fixing the source git revert HEAD --no-edit git push argocd app get shop --refresh A revert is a new commit that undoes an old one. The history keeps the record of what went wrong and when it was undone. There is also argocd app rollback APP ID , which re-applies an earlier entry of the application's history. It only works with automated sync disabled ; otherwise Argo CD refuses ( rollback cannot be initiated when auto-sync is enabled ), because it would immediately sync forward to git again. On automated applications, git is the rollback mechanism. Emergency procedure If you truly must change the cluster by hand during an incident on a self-healing application, first switch the application to manual ( argocd app set APP --sync-policy none ), make the change, and afterwards put the same change into git before re-enabling automation. Otherwise the next sync silently undoes your fix."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"what-argo-cd-ignores","h":"What Argo CD ignores","x":"What Argo CD ignores Argo CD only tracks resources that it created from the application's manifests, plus the fields those manifests specify. Pods created by a Deployment, status fields, and fields set by other controllers are not drift. Objects created by hand in the same namespace are simply not part of the application: they are neither reported nor pruned."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"scenario-1-change-production-with-a-commit","h":"Scenario 1: change production with a commit","x":"Scenario 1: change production with a commit This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"scenario-2-outofsync-who-changed-production","h":"Scenario 2: OutOfSync, who changed production?","x":"Scenario 2: OutOfSync, who changed production? This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Automated sync applies new commits; prune deletes what left git; self-heal reverts manual changes. With these on, a deployment is a commit and a rollback is a git revert . argocd app diff shows the exact difference between live and git. OutOfSync has two causes: an unapplied commit, or drift. The revision tells you which. On a self-healing app, manual fixes vanish. Fix git, or switch to manual first."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz An application has automated sync with self-heal. Someone runs kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=10 . What happens? A. The Deployment stays at 10 and the app shows OutOfSync B. Argo CD commits replicas: 10 to git C. Within seconds Argo CD sets the replica count back to the value in git D. The application is deleted Self-heal treats any deviation from git on a managed field as drift and re-applies the declared state. A manifest file is deleted from the repository. Automated sync is on, prune is off. What is the result? A. The resource stays in the cluster and the app is OutOfSync until someone syncs with prune B. The resource is deleted from the cluster C. Argo CD restores the file in git D. The sync fails Without prune, Argo CD never deletes. It reports that the resource \"requires pruning\". A bad commit broke production on an application with automated sync and self-heal. What is the right way back? A. kubectl rollout undo B. git revert the commit and push C. argocd app rollback with the previous ID D. Delete the pods Git is the source of truth. kubectl rollout undo would be reverted as drift, and argocd app rollback is refused while auto-sync is enabled."},{"l":24,"s":"24-sync-policies","t":"Sync Policies, Drift and Rolling Back with Git","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Automated sync policy Sync options Diffing customization git revert"},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone Argo CD sits on top of everything you have learned: git, Helm, Kubernetes objects, pods. When it reports a problem, the cause can be at any of those levels. This last lesson gives you a map of where each Argo CD error comes from, and ends with an incident that needs the whole course. ⏱ 60 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Classify Argo CD problems by stage (generate, apply, run), fix each kind in the right place, and resolve a multi-layer incident end to end. Why it matters: \"Argo CD is broken\" is almost never true. Argo CD is usually reporting, quite precisely, that a path is wrong, a manifest was rejected, or a pod will not start. Knowing which stage failed sends you to git, to the Application, or to kubectl, and that is the difference between a five-minute fix and an afternoon."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Name the three stages of an Argo CD deployment and the status each failure produces. Resolve a ComparisonError. Resolve a failed sync. Diagnose an application that is Synced but not Healthy. Decide whether a fix belongs in git, in the Application, or in the cluster."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"three-stages-three-kinds-of-failure","h":"Three stages, three kinds of failure","x":"Three stages, three kinds of failure git repo ──(1) GENERATE──► manifests ──(2) APPLY──► cluster objects ──(3) RUN──► working app │ │ │ ComparisonError Sync failed / SyncError Progressing / Degraded Sync Status: Unknown Sync Status: OutOfSync Sync Status: Synced │ │ │ look at: repo URL, revision, look at: the message per look at: pods, with the path, Helm values, YAML resource; namespaces, RBAC, debugging loop of Lesson 15 invalid fields Stage Argo CD shows Meaning Fix belongs in 1. Generate Sync Unknown , condition ComparisonError Argo CD could not produce the desired manifests, so it has nothing to compare. The Application's source settings, or the repository's content 2. Apply Operation phase Failed , condition SyncError Manifests were generated; the cluster refused some of them. The manifest in git, or the destination (namespace, permissions) 3. Run Sync Synced , health Progressing or Degraded Everything was applied; the workload itself is unhealthy. Whatever the pods need: image, config, secrets, resources Always begin with the same two commands: argocd app list # STATUS, HEALTH and CONDITIONS of every application argocd app get APP # conditions with full messages, last operation, per-resource status"},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"stage-1-comparisonerror","h":"Stage 1: ComparisonError","x":"Stage 1: ComparisonError Sync Status: Unknown Health Status: Healthy CONDITION MESSAGE LAST TRANSITION ComparisonError Failed to load target state: failed to generate manifest for 2026-10-11 09:00:00 +0000 UTC source 1 of 1: rpc error: code = Unknown desc = apps/frontend: app path does not exist Skip the boilerplate and read the end of the message: The message ends with Cause Fix PATH: app path does not exist The directory isn't in the repository at that revision (renamed, typo, wrong branch). argocd app set APP --path … unable to resolve 'X' to a commit SHA The branch or tag in targetRevision doesn't exist. argocd app set APP --revision … repository not found / authentication required Wrong URL, or a private repository without credentials. argocd repo add URL --username … --password … helm template … failed … open …values.yaml: no such file A Helm values file listed in the Application doesn't exist at that path. argocd app set APP --values … helm template … failed … nil pointer evaluating … The chart doesn't render with these values (Lesson 21). Fix the chart or values in git error converting YAML to JSON: yaml: line N… A manifest in the repository is not valid YAML. Fix the file in git Why Health says \"Healthy\" during a ComparisonError Health describes the resources Argo CD currently manages. An application that never got past stage 1 manages none, and \"nothing is unhealthy\" reads as Healthy. An existing application that hits a ComparisonError keeps running its last synced state untouched. Either way, look at the conditions, not only at the health column."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"stage-2-the-sync-fails","h":"Stage 2: the sync fails","x":"Stage 2: the sync fails Phase: Failed Message: one or more objects failed to apply, reason: namespaces \"storefront\" not found GROUP KIND NAMESPACE NAME STATUS HEALTH HOOK MESSAGE apps Deployment storefront web OutOfSync Missing namespaces \"storefront\" not found Service storefront web OutOfSync Missing namespaces \"storefront\" not found The per-resource MESSAGE column is exactly what kubectl apply would have printed for that object. You already know these messages: Message Cause Fix namespaces \"X\" not found The destination namespace doesn't exist. --sync-option CreateNamespace=true , or create it … is invalid: spec.… / unknown field … The API server rejected the manifest (Lesson 10). Fix the manifest in git … is forbidden: … cannot create resource … Argo CD's own permissions, or its project, don't allow this resource or destination. The AppProject, or RBAC field is immutable The change can't be applied in place (a selector, a claim template). Delete and recreate that object deliberately A failed automated sync is retried with increasing pauses, but only a change (a new commit, or a corrected setting) will make it succeed."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"stage-3-synced-but-not-healthy","h":"Stage 3: Synced, but not Healthy","x":"Stage 3: Synced, but not Healthy This is the bad-commit signature from Lesson 24, or a dependency the manifests assume and nobody created. Argo CD has done its part. From here it is ordinary Kubernetes debugging: argocd app get APP # which resource is Progressing or Degraded? kubectl -n NS get pods # status kubectl -n NS describe pod NAME # events kubectl -n NS logs NAME # logs Health What Argo CD saw Progressing A Deployment whose rollout hasn't finished, a claim not yet bound, an Ingress without an address. May resolve by itself, or may be stuck. Degraded A definite failure: a Deployment that exceeded its progress deadline (ten minutes by default), a failed Job. Missing A declared resource doesn't exist in the cluster (not yet synced, or deleted by hand). Where does the fix go? The manifest is wrong (image tag, port, selector, resource limit): fix it in git . With self-heal, nothing else lasts. The Application points at the wrong thing (path, revision, values file, namespace): fix the Application , with argocd app set or in the manifest that defines it. Something the app needs is deliberately not in git , typically a Secret with credentials: create it in the cluster (or through the team's secret-management tool). Argo CD doesn't manage it, so it won't remove it."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"scenario-1-comparisonerror","h":"Scenario 1: ComparisonError","x":"Scenario 1: ComparisonError This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"scenario-2-the-hot-fix-that-keeps-disappearing","h":"Scenario 2: the hot-fix that keeps disappearing","x":"Scenario 2: the hot-fix that keeps disappearing This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"capstone-launch-day","h":"Capstone: launch day","x":"Capstone: launch day Everything in this course in one incident: three applications, three faults in three different stages, Helm values, a missing secret, a Service that points nowhere, an Ingress, and self-healing that will undo any shortcut. Take it one application at a time. For each, decide the stage first, then where the fix belongs. This interactive scenario needs JavaScript."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"the-method-one-last-time","h":"The method, one last time","x":"The method, one last time You have used the same approach on a single container, a Compose stack, pods, Services, Helm releases and Argo CD applications: Look at the state before touching anything: docker ps -a , kubectl get , helm history , argocd app list . Find the message. Every layer writes down why it stopped: logs, events, conditions. Identify the layer the message belongs to, and therefore where a fix must go. Change one thing , in the place where the change will last. Test again , and read the next message. The tools will keep changing. That loop will not."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Generate → apply → run. ComparisonError, sync failure and unhealthy workloads each belong to one stage. Sync Unknown with ComparisonError: the source (path, revision, values, YAML). Read the end of the message. Sync failed: the per-resource message is the API server's own answer. Synced but not Healthy: Argo CD is done; debug the pods as usual. Manifests are fixed in git, pointers in the Application, and intentionally unmanaged prerequisites in the cluster."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz An application shows Sync Status Unknown and a ComparisonError ending in \"app path does not exist\". What should you check? A. The application's path against the directories in the repository at that revision B. The pods in the destination namespace C. The cluster's free capacity D. The image registry A ComparisonError means manifests could not be generated. The pods are not involved yet. An application is Synced and has been Progressing for fifteen minutes; one pod is in ImagePullBackOff. Auto-sync with self-heal is on. Where do you fix it? A. kubectl set image on the Deployment B. argocd app sync C. Delete the pod D. Correct the image reference in git and push The cluster matches git, so git contains the wrong tag. A kubectl change would be reverted, and a sync would re-apply the same mistake. A database pod managed by Argo CD is in CreateContainerConfigError because the Secret db-auth doesn't exist. The team keeps credentials out of git on purpose. What is the appropriate fix? A. Commit the Secret with the password to the repository B. Disable self-heal C. Create the Secret in the cluster (or via the team's secret manager); Argo CD does not manage it and will leave it alone D. Remove the reference from the Deployment Not everything belongs in git. Prerequisites that are intentionally unmanaged must be provided in the cluster, by a controlled process."},{"l":25,"s":"25-troubleshooting-argocd","t":"Troubleshooting Argo CD and the Capstone","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Argo CD troubleshooting tools Resource health Helm in Argo CD Secret management Kubernetes: monitoring, logging and debugging"}];
